#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:241
#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:79
msgid "Tuples"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:243
msgid "Records `(A, B)` are first-class values with numeric fields:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:245
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn divmod(a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, i32) {\n"
"    return (a / b, a % b);\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"tuples\");\n"
"    let (q, r) = divmod(17, 5);   // destructuring\n"
"    let t = (1, true);\n"
"    log.info(f\"q={q} r={r} t.0={t.0}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:264
msgid ""
"The pair is also rut's standard error channel — the next chapters use it "
"constantly."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:267 src/tutorial/control-flow.md:312
#: src/tutorial/functions.md:242 src/tutorial/types.md:278
#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:400 src/tutorial/errors.md:233
#: src/tutorial/modules.md:257 src/tutorial/async.md:185
#: src/tutorial/stdlib.md:396
msgid "Put it together"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:269
msgid ""
"Put this in a module directory and run it: `literals/rut.jsonc` names the "
"two packages the program uses, and the program is `literals/main.rut`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:273
msgid ""
"```jsonc\n"
"// literals/rut.jsonc\n"
"{\n"
"  \"name\": \"literals\",\n"
"  \"entry\": { \"lib\": \"./main.rut\" },\n"
"\n"
"  \"deps\": {\n"
"    \"pouch\": { \"url\": "
"\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/hpp2334/rut@std-v5/dist/std/pouch.rutbundle\", "
"\"sha256\": "
"\"3ceedd188659972ff6c797289e57e9fc9a686da9ea55f49ceb28ce4127c6ac80\" },\n"
"    \"ink\":   { \"url\": "
"\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/hpp2334/rut@std-v5/dist/std/ink.rutbundle\", "
"\"sha256\": "
"\"200ab1ea41bee7145d20c0665f799302436dcbe82bdab8858c7c74708d1b306a\" }\n"
"  }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:286
msgid "Save the program as `literals/main.rut` and run `rut run literals`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:288
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: f32; y: f32 }\n"
"\n"
"fn literals(name: str) {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"literals\");\n"
"    let a = 10;                      // i32 (default)\n"
"    let b = 10u8;                    // u8 via suffix\n"
"    let c: u64 = 10;                 // u64 via annotation — fits the "
"default\n"
"    let big = 18446744073709551615u64; // past the `i32` default: suffix "
"REQUIRED\n"
"    let cast = 300 as u8;            // 44 — `as` truncates\n"
"    let d = 1.5;                     // f32 (default)\n"
"    let e = 1.5f32;                  // f32 via suffix\n"
"    let d64: f64 = 1.5;              // f64 via annotation\n"
"    let s = \"hi\\tname\";              // plain string: escapes processed\n"
"    let raw = r\"C:\\temp\\log.txt\";    // raw literal: NO escape "
"processing\n"
"    let t = f\"hi {name}!\";           // format literal\n"
"    let ch = \"h\";                    // a 1-codepoint str\n"
"    let mut arr = [1, 2, 3];         // [i32] — fixed array\n"
"    let mut zero: Vec<f32> = Vec<f32>.filled(0.0, 1024);  // growable, flat\n"
"    let grow = Vec<i32>.from([1, 2, 3]);  // array -> growable\n"
"    arr[0] = 7;                      // element write needs `mut`\n"
"    zero[0] = 9.0f32;\n"
"    let bin = bytes(64);             // 64 zeroed octets\n"
"    let p = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };    // struct literal: no `new`\n"
"\n"
"    log.info(f\"a={a} e={e} d64={d64} ch={ch} p.x={p.x} zero[0]={zero[0]} "
"len={grow.len()} bin={bin.len()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    literals(\"rut\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/values-and-variables.md:328
msgid "Next: [control flow and `when`](control-flow.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:3
msgid ""
"rut has the classic structured statements — `if`/`else`, `while`, `for` — "
"and exactly one match construct: `when`, an exhaustive pattern _expression_. "
"The full rules live in [the reference on control "
"flow](../reference/control-flow.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:8
msgid "`if` and `else`"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:10
msgid "Braces are always required, whatever the body length:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:12
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"flow\");\n"
"    for (let hits of [0, 5, 12]) {\n"
"        let mut first = false;\n"
"        let mut warm = false;\n"
"        let mut hot = false;\n"
"        if (hits == 0) {\n"
"            first = true;\n"
"        } else if (hits < 10) {\n"
"            warm = true;\n"
"        } else {\n"
"            hot = true;\n"
"        }\n"
"        log.info(f\"hits={hits} first={first} warm={warm} hot={hot}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:39
msgid ""
"The condition must be a `bool` — there is no truthiness coercion, and no "
"parenthesized-assignment footgun."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:42
msgid "Loops"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:44
msgid "**`while`** repeats until its condition is false:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:46
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"sieve\");\n"
"    let i = 2;\n"
"    let limit = 12;\n"
"    let mut marks = [0; 16];\n"
"    let mut m = i * i;\n"
"    while (m <= limit) {\n"
"        marks[m] = 1;\n"
"        m += i;\n"
"    }\n"
"    log.info(f\"marks[4]={marks[4]} marks[5]={marks[5]} stopped at "
"m={m}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:67
msgid ""
"**`for..of`** iterates anything with elements: fixed arrays, `Vec`s, `str` "
"(yielding one-codepoint `str`s), `bytes` (yielding `u8`s), and any type that "
"marks an `[iterable]` member (a user class or an enum — the loop calls that "
"ONE designated member; see [interfaces](../reference/interfaces.md)):"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:73
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"loops\");\n"
"    let mut n = 0;\n"
"    for (let w of [\"rut\", \"runs\", \"rut\"]) {\n"
"        // w is a str\n"
"    }\n"
"    for (let c of \"héllo\") {\n"
"        n += 1;             // 5 iterations — codepoints, not the 6 bytes\n"
"    }\n"
"    log.info(f\"n={n}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:93
msgid "The loop variable is always spelled `let` and is fresh each iteration."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:95
msgid ""
"**Indexed `for`** is the C shape. The induction variable is _loop-owned_: "
"the update clause (and the body) may assign it without `mut`."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:98
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"loops\");\n"
"    let xs = [3, 1, 4];\n"
"    let n = xs.len();\n"
"    let mut total = 0;\n"
"    for (let i = 0; i < n; i += 1) {\n"
"        total += xs[i];\n"
"    }\n"
"    log.info(f\"total={total}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:117
msgid ""
"**`break` and `continue`** work in every loop form; there are no labels and "
"no `do..while`."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:120 src/reference/lexical-structure.md:44
msgid "`return`"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:122
msgid ""
"`return expr;` exits the function. If the function returns nothing, the "
"arrow is omitted and `return;` (or falling off the end) returns:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:125
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn swap(mut xs: Vec<i32>, a: i32, b: i32) {\n"
"    let t = xs[a];\n"
"    xs[a] = xs[b];\n"
"    xs[b] = t;\n"
"    // no return — the function's type is nil\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"swap\");\n"
"    let xs = Vec<i32>.from([1, 2, 3]);\n"
"    swap(xs, 0, 2);\n"
"    log.info(f\"first={xs[0]} last={xs[2]}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:148
msgid "`when` — the one match"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:150
msgid ""
"`when (value) { pattern -> body, ... }` is an expression: the first matching "
"arm produces the value. No fallthrough — exactly one arm runs."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:153
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn classify(n: i32) -> str {\n"
"    return when (n) {\n"
"        0       -> \"zero\",\n"
"        1, 2, 3 -> \"small\",     // comma-separated alternatives\n"
"        else    -> \"big\",       // the wildcard\n"
"    };\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"when\");\n"
"    for (let n of [0, 2, 9]) {\n"
"        log.info(f\"{n}: {classify(n)}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:178
msgid "The arm body before the `,` is an expression; arms can also be blocks:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:180
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Light { Green, Yellow, Red }\n"
"\n"
"fn go()    { Logger.new(\"light\").info(\"go\"); }\n"
"fn brake() { Logger.new(\"light\").info(\"brake\"); }\n"
"fn stop()  { Logger.new(\"light\").info(\"stop\"); }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let l = Light.Yellow;\n"
"    when (l) {\n"
"        Light.Green  -> { go(); },\n"
"        Light.Yellow -> { brake(); },\n"
"        Light.Red    -> { stop(); },\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:203
msgid ""
"Used as a statement, a `when` must produce nothing — the block arms above "
"are statement arms."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:206 src/reference/control-flow.md:67
msgid "Patterns"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:208
msgid ""
"v1 patterns are: enum members (`Light.Red`), literals (integers, floats, "
"`bool`, `str`), comma-separated alternatives, and `else`. There are no "
"ranges, no destructuring, and no guards — an `if` inside the arm body does "
"that job."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:213
msgid "Exhaustiveness is enforced"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:215
msgid ""
"**Enum scrutinee:** either cover every member or add `else`. A partial "
"`when` over an enum is a compile error — when you add a member later, the "
"compiler finds every `when` you must extend."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:218
msgid ""
"**Any other scrutinee:** `else` is mandatory (integers and strings cannot be "
"enumerated)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:221
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Light { Green, Yellow, Red }\n"
"\n"
"fn go()    { Logger.new(\"light\").info(\"go\"); }\n"
"fn brake() { Logger.new(\"light\").info(\"brake\"); }\n"
"fn stop()  { Logger.new(\"light\").info(\"stop\"); }\n"
"\n"
"fn drive(l: Light) {\n"
"    when (l) {\n"
"        Light.Green  -> { go(); },\n"
"        Light.Yellow -> { brake(); },\n"
"        Light.Red    -> { stop(); },   // all members: no else needed\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn describe(n: i32) -> str {\n"
"    return when (n) {\n"
"        0    -> \"zero\",\n"
"        else -> \"not zero\",            // non-enum scrutinee: else "
"REQUIRED\n"
"    };\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    drive(Light.Red);\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"light\");\n"
"    log.info(describe(0));\n"
"    log.info(describe(7));\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:259
msgid "`when` over a `bool` reads nicely as a two-arm check:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:261
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32 }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"nullable\");\n"
"    let p: ?Point = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n"
"    when (p != nil) {\n"
"        true -> { log.info(f\"point {p.x} {p.y}\"); },\n"
"        else -> { log.info(\"point: nil\"); },\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:280
msgid ""
"Duplicate patterns — and arms made unreachable by an earlier one — are "
"compile errors."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:283
msgid "One type across the arms"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:285
msgid ""
"All arm expressions must agree: that shared type is the `when`'s type. A "
"`when` that builds a value usually reads best wrapped in a function — the "
"function's return type then pins every arm:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:289
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Light { Green, Yellow, Red }\n"
"\n"
"fn light_for(i: i32) -> Light {\n"
"    return when (i) {\n"
"        0    -> Light.Green,\n"
"        1    -> Light.Yellow,\n"
"        else -> Light.Red,\n"
"    };\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"light\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"{light_for(0)} {light_for(1)} {light_for(5)}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:314
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Light { Green, Yellow, Red }\n"
"\n"
"fn action(l: Light) -> str {\n"
"    return when (l) {\n"
"        Light.Green  -> \"go\",\n"
"        Light.Yellow -> \"brake\",\n"
"        Light.Red    -> \"stop\",\n"
"    };\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn light_for(i: i32) -> Light {\n"
"    return when (i) {\n"
"        0    -> Light.Green,\n"
"        1    -> Light.Yellow,\n"
"        else -> Light.Red,\n"
"    };\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn classify(n: i32) -> str {\n"
"    return when (n) {\n"
"        0       -> \"zero\",\n"
"        1, 2, 3 -> \"small\",\n"
"        else    -> \"big\",\n"
"    };\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"lights\");\n"
"\n"
"    for (let n of [0, 2, 9]) {\n"
"        log.info(f\"{n}: {classify(n)}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    let mut i = 0;\n"
"    while (i < 3) {\n"
"        log.info(action(light_for(i)));\n"
"        i += 1;\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/control-flow.md:367
msgid "Next: [functions, closures, and generics](functions.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:3
msgid ""
"Functions are declared with `fn`, values can be functions, and generic "
"functions monomorphize at compile time — each instantiation gets its own "
"specialized code. The details live in [the reference on functions, closures, "
"and generics](../reference/functions-closures-generics.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:8
msgid "Declaring functions"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:10
msgid ""
"Parameter and return types are always written. A function whose return type "
"is omitted returns nothing:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:13
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use calc::{ Math };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: f32; y: f32 }\n"
"\n"
"fn swap(mut xs: Vec<i32>, a: i32, b: i32) {\n"
"    let t = xs[a];\n"
"    xs[a] = xs[b];\n"
"    xs[b] = t;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn length(pt: Point) -> f64 {\n"
"    return Math.sqrt((pt.x * pt.x + pt.y * pt.y) as f64);\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"fns\");\n"
"    let xs = Vec<i32>.from([1, 2, 3]);\n"
"    swap(xs, 0, 2);\n"
"    log.info(f\"first={xs[0]} length={length(Point { x: 3, y: 4 })}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:42
msgid ""
"A `mut` parameter (`fn step(mut p: Point)`) is the callee's permission to "
"write through `p` — and because non-primitive values share, that means the "
"caller's value. A plain parameter promises not to write through it."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:47
msgid ""
"Recursion works as expected — functions are visible in their own bodies, and "
"mutual recursion within a module needs no forward declaration."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:51
msgid "Anonymous functions"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:53
msgid ""
"The closure spelling is an anonymous `fn` with a block body. There is no "
"arrow shorthand — every closure states its parameter and return types, which "
"is what lets it inhabit a first-class `fn` type:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:57
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"closures\");\n"
"    let add = fn (a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { return a + b; };\n"
"\n"
"    let area_of = fn (r: f32) -> f32 {\n"
"        let sq = r * r;\n"
"        return sq * 3.14159265f32;\n"
"    };\n"
"\n"
"    add(1, 2);          // call it like any function\n"
"    log.info(f\"add={add(1, 2)} area={area_of(1)}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:78
msgid ""
"Function types are written `fn(P..) -> R` and appear anywhere a type does:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:81
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn apply(f: fn(i32) -> i32, v: i32) -> i32 {\n"
"    return f(v);\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"fns\");\n"
"    let double = fn (x: i32) -> i32 { return x * 2; };\n"
"    log.info(f\"apply={apply(double, 21)}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:99
msgid ""
"Closures capture the enclosing bindings — a closure body sees and can use "
"the locals around it, including after the enclosing function would have "
"returned, because captured cells stay alive as long as the closure does. The "
"capture law has two halves: primitives (and `nil` and `fn` values) copy — "
"the closure gets its own slot, and a reassign inside the closure never moves "
"the original — while everything ref-headed (a `Vec`, a struct, `str`, `?T`, "
"…) crosses as a handle to the same cell. That second half includes "
"whole-value reassignment: a captured binding that is reassigned — either "
"frame — shares its slot with the closure, so both sides always see the "
"current value:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:110
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() -> nil {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"cap\");\n"
"    let mut xs: Vec<i32> = Vec.new();\n"
"    xs.push(1);\n"
"    let len = fn() -> i32 { return xs.len(); };\n"
"    let mut ys: Vec<i32> = Vec.new();\n"
"    ys.push(9); ys.push(9);\n"
"    xs = ys;                       // a rebind the closure sees\n"
"    log.info(f\"len={len()}\");      // 2 — the closure shares the slot\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:126
#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:78
msgid "Generics"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:128
msgid ""
"A generic function declares type parameters in angle brackets. Each concrete "
"instantiation is compiled separately (monomorphized), so generics cost "
"nothing at runtime:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:132
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn first<T>(xs: [T], fallback: T) -> T {\n"
"    if (xs.len() == 0) {\n"
"        return fallback;\n"
"    }\n"
"    return xs[0];\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"generics\");\n"
"    let head = first([10, 20], -1);      // first<i32> — inferred\n"
"    let name = first([\"a\", \"b\"], \"?\");   // first<str> — a separate "
"instance\n"
"    log.info(f\"head={head} name={name}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:154
msgid ""
"`T` is inferred from the arguments; you rarely spell `first<i32>(..)` "
"explicitly. Two rules to know:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:157
msgid ""
"A bare `T` has **no methods** — you cannot call anything on a value whose "
"type is just `T`. Pass concrete values in, spell an interface bound (`T "
"requires Labeled`), or take an interface-typed parameter (next chapter)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:161
msgid ""
"In v1 a _generic class_ in a parameter position does not unify — `fn sum(xs: "
"Vec<i32>)` is fine, but a function generic over `T` taking `Vec<T>` is not "
"yet the shape to reach for. Concrete instantiations cover most code."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:166
msgid "`requires` bounds"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:168
msgid "An inline `requires` bound gates which instantiations compile:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:170
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"interface Labeled {\n"
"    fn label(self) -> str;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"struct Tag { id: i32 }\n"
"\n"
"impl Tag {\n"
"    fn label(self) -> str { return f\"tag-{self.id}\"; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn name<T requires Labeled>(x: T) -> str {\n"
"    let w: Labeled = x;     // the bound proves this widening\n"
"    return w.label();\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"bounds\");\n"
"    log.info(name(Tag { id: 7 }));\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:198
msgid ""
"The bound is _admission-only_: it checks at each call site that the concrete "
"type satisfies the named interface (or union of type names), and it proves a "
"`T`\\-typed value may be used as that interface type — it does not put "
"methods on `T` itself. Union bounds admit any member:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:207
msgid ""
"This is exactly how typed JSON entry points are spelled — `decodeJson<T "
"requires JsonDeserialize>(s: str)` — see [errors and "
"optionality](errors.md). Bounds and unions are covered fully in [the "
"reference on type aliases and union bounds](../reference/type-aliases.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:212
msgid "Free functions are the default idiom"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:214
msgid ""
"rut's records carry no methods (bodies are fields only; methods live in "
"`impl` blocks — see [structs, enums, and classes](types.md)), so the "
"everyday shape is small data plus free functions over it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:218
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: f32; y: f32 }\n"
"\n"
"fn nudged(pt: Point) -> Point {\n"
"    return Point { x: pt.x + 1, y: pt.y };\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"free-fns\");\n"
"    let p = nudged(Point { x: 2, y: 5 });\n"
"    log.info(f\"p.x={p.x} p.y={p.y}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:238
msgid ""
"Reach for methods when something is genuinely _the receiver's_ behavior, and "
"for the members other code observes through interfaces — everything else is "
"a plain function."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:244
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn first<T>(xs: [T], fallback: T) -> T {\n"
"    if (xs.len() == 0) {\n"
"        return fallback;\n"
"    }\n"
"    return xs[0];\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn sum(xs: Vec<i32>) -> i32 {\n"
"    let mut total = 0;\n"
"    for (let x of xs) {\n"
"        total += x;\n"
"    }\n"
"    return total;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"closures\");\n"
"    let add = fn (a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { return a + b; };\n"
"    let area_of = fn (r: f32) -> f32 {\n"
"        let sq = r * r;\n"
"        return sq * 3.14159265f32;\n"
"    };\n"
"    log.info(f\"add={add(1, 2)} area={area_of(1)} sum={sum(Vec<i32>.from([1, "
"2, 3]))}\");\n"
"\n"
"    let head = first([10, 20], -1);       // first<i32> — monomorphized\n"
"    let name = first([\"a\", \"b\"], \"?\");    // first<str> — separate "
"instance\n"
"    log.info(f\"head={head} name={name}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/functions.md:283
msgid "Next: [structs, enums, and classes](types.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:3
msgid "rut has three user-defined type forms, each with one job:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:5
msgid "**`enum`** — a small set of named constants."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:6
msgid ""
"**`struct`** — an open data record: every field public, literal construction "
"everywhere."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:8
msgid ""
"**`class`** — a sealed record: private fields, construction through class "
"methods only."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:11
msgid ""
"In all three, **the type body is fields only** — every method lives in an "
"`impl` block. A `fn` written inside a type body is a parse error. The "
"reference pages are [structs](../reference/structs.md), "
"[enums](../reference/enums.md), and [classes and "
"constructors](../reference/classes.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:20
msgid ""
"An enum is a distinct named type over integer constants. No payloads and no "
"computed members — where another language would use a union of literal "
"strings, rut uses an enum:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:24
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Light { Green, Yellow, Red }\n"
"\n"
"enum Direction { Up = 1, Down, Left, Right }   // 1, 2, 3, 4\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"enums\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"{Light.Green} {Direction.Left} {Direction.Right}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:41
msgid ""
"Members are the enum's values and are spelled qualified: `Light.Green`. "
"Explicit initializers set where the numbering starts; the rest continue from "
"there."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:45
msgid ""
"`when` over an enum must be exhaustive — every member, or an `else` arm (see "
"[control flow and when](control-flow.md)). Enums render as their member name "
"in format strings. Enums take `impl` blocks — non-self methods on the name, "
"`self` methods on a value (a marked `[iterable] fn` makes `for (let v of l)` "
"walk) — see [enums](../reference/enums.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:52
msgid "Structs — open records"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:54
msgid ""
"Declare with `struct`, construct with a literal — anywhere in a function "
"body, nested inside other literals. Construction is the literal; there is no "
"constructor gate:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:58
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point {\n"
"    x: f32;\n"
"    y: f32;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"struct Rect {\n"
"    min: Point;\n"
"    max: Point;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"struct Style {\n"
"    color: u32 = 0xff00ff;      // field initializer: literals may omit it\n"
"    width: f32 = 1;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"structs\");\n"
"    let p = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };                    // every field, by "
"name\n"
"    let s = Style {};                                // defaults fill the "
"rest\n"
"    let r = Rect { min: Point { x: 0, y: 0 }, max: p };\n"
"    log.info(f\"r.max.x={r.max.x} width={s.width}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:89
msgid ""
"All fields are public, always — member visibility in a struct is a compile "
"error. Privacy is what classes are for. Methods live in `impl` blocks with "
"real visibility — `pub fn` exports cross-module, plain `fn` stays "
"module-private (see [structs](../reference/structs.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:95
msgid ""
"**Structs share.** A struct value is a handle to a heap cell: assignment, "
"arguments, and returns all pass the handle, and a write through any alias is "
"visible through all of them. Writing needs a `mut` binding or `mut` "
"parameter:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:100
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: f32; y: f32 }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"sharing\");\n"
"    let mut p = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n"
"    let q = p;              // q and p name ONE cell\n"
"    p.x = 4;                // q.x is 4 now\n"
"    log.info(f\"q.x={q.x} same cell: {q == p}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:118
msgid ""
"`==` on struct values is **cell identity** — `q == p` is true (one cell), `p "
"== Point { x: 4, y: 2 }` is false (a different cell). To compare field by "
"field, write a function."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:122
msgid ""
"Recursive shapes are legal — a record may name itself through a nullable "
"field, because `?Node` is one word:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:125
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Node {\n"
"    value: i32;\n"
"    left: ?Node;\n"
"    right: ?Node;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn count(n: ?Node) -> i32 {\n"
"    let mut c = 1;\n"
"    if (n.left != nil) { c += count(n.left); }\n"
"    if (n.right != nil) { c += count(n.right); }\n"
"    return c;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"nodes\");\n"
"    let n = Node {\n"
"        value: 1,\n"
"        left: Node { value: 2, left: nil, right: nil },\n"
"        right: nil,\n"
"    };\n"
"    log.info(f\"count={count(n)}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:156
msgid "Classes — sealed records"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:158
msgid ""
"A class adds two things to a struct: module-private fields and construction "
"gated through class methods. There is no outside literal — the only way to "
"build a class value from outside is to call a class method that chooses to. "
"(Cleanup hooks are not a class privilege: mark a `[disposal]` member on "
"either shape, and the engine calls it when the value's cell refcount reaches "
"zero — see [Rc, dispose, and identity](../reference/rc-dispose-identity.md).)"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:166
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"class Counter {\n"
"    n: i32 = 0;             // module-private — the class's business\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Counter {\n"
"    pub fn new() -> Self {                 // the construction surface\n"
"        return Self { };                   // the class-private literal\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn press(mut self) {\n"
"        self.n = self.n.wrapping_add(1);\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn count(self) -> i32 { return self.n; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"counter\");\n"
"    let c = Counter.new();\n"
"    c.press();\n"
"    c.press();\n"
"    log.info(f\"count={c.count()}\");      // 2\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:198
msgid "The pieces:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:200
msgid ""
"**A class method is just a function without `self`.** `Rect.new(w, h)`, "
"`Version.parse(s)`, `Rect.from_square(s)` — any no-`self` method returning "
"`Self` is a constructor. `new` is a convention, not syntax."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:203
msgid ""
"**The `Self { .. }` literal is class-private** — legal anywhere in the "
"class's own impl block, never outside. This is the seal."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:205
msgid ""
"**Instance methods spell `self` explicitly** as the first parameter; `mut "
"self` marks methods that write. A computed property is a method "
"(`c.count()`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:208
msgid ""
"**Construction is validation.** A constructor is an ordinary function — it "
"can check arguments and refuse:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:211
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"class Rect {\n"
"    w: f32;\n"
"    h: f32;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Rect {\n"
"    pub fn new(w: f32, h: f32) -> Self {\n"
"        if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) {\n"
"            panic(\"Rect: negative extents\");\n"
"        }\n"
"        return Self { w: w, h: h };\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn from_square(s: f32) -> Self {\n"
"        return Rect.new(s, s);\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f32 { return self.w * self.h; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"rect\");\n"
"    let r = Rect.new(3, 4);\n"
"    let sq = Rect.from_square(2);\n"
"    log.info(f\"area={r.area()} square={sq.area()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:246
msgid ""
"A _try_\\-constructor answers the nullable — `nil` is a failed validation, "
"not a crash:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:249
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"impl Version {\n"
"    pub fn parse(s: str) -> ?Version {\n"
"        // ... split \"1.2\" — on failure:\n"
"        return nil;\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:258
msgid ""
"**No inheritance.** There is no `extends`, no `super`, no overriding. Code "
"sharing is composition (hold a helper in a field) or free functions; "
"polymorphism is structural interfaces — the next chapter."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:262
msgid "Visibility recap"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:264
msgid "Struct members: public, always."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:265
msgid ""
"Class members: unannotated is module-private; `pub` exposes to importers "
"(also `pub(mod)`, `pub(super)`, `pub(self)`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:267
msgid ""
"The types themselves follow the same rule: `pub class Vec<T>` is importable, "
"a bare `class Helper` stays in its module. See [modules and "
"packages](modules.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:271
msgid "Struct or class?"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:273
msgid ""
"Default to a struct: data in, data out, shared like every other value. Reach "
"for a class when the type has rules that construction must enforce, holds "
"private state that outsiders must not read, or owns a resource that needs "
"explicit cleanup."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:280
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"class Counter {\n"
"    n: i32 = 0;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Counter {\n"
"    pub fn new() -> Self {\n"
"        return Self { };\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn press(mut self) {\n"
"        self.n = self.n.wrapping_add(1);\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn count(self) -> i32 { return self.n; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class Rect {\n"
"    w: f32;\n"
"    h: f32;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Rect {\n"
"    pub fn new(w: f32, h: f32) -> Self {\n"
"        if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) {\n"
"            panic(\"Rect: negative extents\");\n"
"        }\n"
"        return Self { w: w, h: h };\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn from_square(s: f32) -> Self {\n"
"        return Rect.new(s, s);\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f32 { return self.w * self.h; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"classes\");\n"
"    let c = Counter.new();\n"
"    c.press();\n"
"    c.press();\n"
"    let r = Rect.new(3, 4);\n"
"    let sq = Rect.from_square(2.0f32);\n"
"    log.info(f\"count={c.count()} area={r.area()} square={sq.area()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/types.md:334
msgid "Next: [interfaces and impl blocks](interfaces.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:3
msgid ""
"An interface declares a set of method signatures; a type satisfies it by "
"**having the members**. Satisfaction is **structural** — there is no "
"admission step, no registration, no `impl Interface for Type` anywhere in "
"the language: `impl T { .. }` (the type's own inherent impl) is the only "
"impl form, and the boundary check is the whole law. The "
"one-mechanism-per-job philosophy behind this is in [interfaces and "
"dispatch](../core-concepts/interfaces-and-dispatch.md); the full story is in "
"[the reference on interfaces](../reference/interfaces.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:13
msgid "Declaring an interface"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:15
msgid ""
"Interfaces declare method signatures — no bodies, no default "
"implementations, no fields:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:25
msgid ""
"Methods spell their receiver `self` explicitly, like every rut method. "
"Anything that looks like a property is a method: declare `fn count(self) -> "
"i32;`, not a field. `Self` and generics are legal in signatures; interface "
"members carry no `pub` — they are as visible as the interface — and the "
"interface itself takes no bounds and embeds nothing: a flat member set, "
"compose by declaring the members."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:32
msgid "Satisfying an interface"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:34
msgid ""
"Satisfaction is membership. Give the type a `pub` member for every interface "
"member, on the type's own inherent impl, and it satisfies — nothing else to "
"write:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:38
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub interface Shape {\n"
"    fn area(self) -> f64;\n"
"    fn name(self) -> str;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class Circle { r: f64; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Circle {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return 3.14159 * self.r * self.r; }\n"
"    pub fn name(self) -> str { return \"circle\"; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"interfaces\");\n"
"    let c = Circle { r: 1.0 };\n"
"    log.info(f\"{c.name()}={c.area()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:64
msgid "Rules worth knowing:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:66
msgid ""
"**Signatures must match** — receiver form (`self` vs `mut self` vs none), "
"parameter types, return type, and `async` spelling, exactly. A member that "
"is almost right is not right: the diagnostic names the first mismatching "
"member."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:70
msgid ""
"**Members live on the type's own inherent impl**, in the type's module. "
"`impl Circle { pub fn area(self) -> f64 { .. } }` is where the member set "
"comes from; there is no second impl form to reach for."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:74
msgid ""
"**Only the type's own public members count.** A used package's type "
"satisfies through the members its own module spells; you cannot add members "
"to a foreign type, and nothing you write can register a satisfaction for a "
"type that lacks the members."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:78
msgid ""
"**Shape-equal is satisfied** — including by types whose author never thought "
"of your interface. Two types with the same member shapes both satisfy, and "
"there is no opt-out. The convention that keeps this honest: give near-miss "
"members distinct names or shapes, so accidental satisfaction stays a "
"non-event in practice."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:83
msgid ""
"**An interface with no members is satisfied by everything** — the empty "
"member set is vacuously true. There is no marker idiom: if a declaration "
"needs to mean something, it spells a member."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:87
msgid "Inherent impls"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:89
msgid ""
"`impl Type { .. }` is where a type's own methods live: class methods like "
"`new`, instance methods, helpers. It compiles only in the module that "
"declares the type. A generic type's inherent impl declares its binders up "
"front: `impl<T> Vec<T> { .. }`."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:94
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"class Circle {\n"
"    r: f64;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Circle {\n"
"    pub fn new(r: f64) -> Self {\n"
"        return Self { r: r };\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"interfaces\");\n"
"    let c = Circle.new(1.0);\n"
"    log.info(f\"r={c.r}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:118
msgid ""
"Every member the satisfaction law reads comes from here — public members of "
"the type's inherent impl, and nothing else."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:121
msgid "Using interface values"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:123
msgid ""
"An interface name in type position is the bare name. Four forms, one law "
"each — checking at the boundary, never a search:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:126
msgid ""
"**Interface-typed parameters accept any satisfying type** — write the "
"function once, call it with each concrete type:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:129
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub interface Shape {\n"
"    fn area(self) -> f64;\n"
"    fn name(self) -> str;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class Circle { r: f64; }\n"
"class Square { side: f64; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Circle {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return 3.14159 * self.r * self.r; }\n"
"    pub fn name(self) -> str { return \"circle\"; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Square {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return self.side * self.side; }\n"
"    pub fn name(self) -> str { return \"square\"; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn describe(s: Shape) -> str {\n"
"    return f\"{s.name()}={s.area()}\";\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"interfaces\");\n"
"    log.info(describe(Circle { r: 1.0 }));    // Circle satisfies: checked "
"here\n"
"    log.info(describe(Square { side: 3.0 })); // and here\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:166
msgid ""
"The check fires where the concrete value crosses into the interface — each "
"call above verifies the argument's member set once. A type without the "
"members is refused at that same spot, and the diagnostic names the type, the "
"interface, and the missing member:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:186
msgid ""
"**Typed lets are satisfaction assertions** — the annotation is the question, "
"the initializer's member set is the answer:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:189
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub interface Shape {\n"
"    fn area(self) -> f64;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class Square { side: f64; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Square {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return self.side * self.side; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"interfaces\");\n"
"    let s: Shape = Square { side: 2.0 };   // asserted and checked\n"
"    log.info(f\"area={s.area()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:213
msgid ""
"**Heterogeneous containers** hold mixed satisfiers behind the interface "
"name. Inside a single-typed context calls bind directly; iterating a "
"collection of `Shape` consults each value's itable — the compiler picks, and "
"mis-guessing costs one hop, never wrong behavior:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:218
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub interface Shape {\n"
"    fn area(self) -> f64;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class Circle { r: f64; }\n"
"class Square { side: f64; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Circle {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return 3.14159 * self.r * self.r; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Square {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return self.side * self.side; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"interfaces\");\n"
"    let mut shapes: Vec<Shape> = Vec.new();   // each push checks\n"
"    shapes.push(Circle { r: 1.0 });\n"
"    shapes.push(Square { side: 3.0 });\n"
"    let mut total: f64 = 0.0;\n"
"    for (let s of shapes) {\n"
"        total += s.area();       // dispatches per element\n"
"    }\n"
"    log.info(f\"total={total} n={shapes.len()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:254
msgid "Primitives ride in wrappers"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:256
msgid ""
"Primitives, arrays, tuples, and `?T` have no inherent members, so they can "
"never satisfy — and no layer will invent a satisfaction for you. The route "
"in is a **newtype class**: a one-field wrapper whose inherent impl carries "
"the members, spelled at every call site:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:261
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub interface Show {\n"
"    fn show(self) -> str;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class NumI64(i64);\n"
"\n"
"impl NumI64 {\n"
"    pub fn show(self) -> str { return str(self.inner); }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn print_it(x: Show) -> str { return x.show(); }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"interfaces\");\n"
"    log.info(print_it(NumI64(64)));    // spelled manufacture\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:286
msgid ""
"`print_it(NumI64(64))` — ✓ spelled, sound, per-call. `print_it(64)` — ✗ "
"forever: no auto-insertion exists. Wrappers compose — a generic wrapper "
"`class Box<T requires Show> { v: T; }` checks its bound at instantiation, "
"and its own members let `Box` satisfy in turn. The standard library's json "
"and flow packages are built exactly this way (see [the standard "
"library](stdlib.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:294
msgid "Type tests: `is`"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:296
msgid "`expr is Type` answers with a `bool` and never traps:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:298
msgid "**Concrete RHS** — `p is Circle`: an exact-type test."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:299
msgid ""
"**Interface RHS** — `p is Shape`: a capability probe — true when the value's "
"concrete type was satisfaction-boxed somewhere (itable fills exist for "
"`Shape`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:303
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub interface Shape {\n"
"    fn area(self) -> f64;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class Circle { r: f64; }\n"
"class Square { side: f64; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Circle {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return 3.14159 * self.r * self.r; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Square {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return self.side * self.side; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"interfaces\");\n"
"    let mut shapes: Vec<Shape> = Vec.new();\n"
"    shapes.push(Circle { r: 1.0 });\n"
"    shapes.push(Square { side: 2.0 });\n"
"    let sq = Square { side: 2.0 };\n"
"    log.info(f\"shape: {sq is Shape} circle: {sq is Circle}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:336
msgid ""
"`is` answers the question; it changes nothing — there is no narrowing and no "
"downcast through an interface. An interface-typed value is used through its "
"interface's members; if you need the erased-storage version — a value whose "
"type is _forgotten_ until recovered — that is `opaque`, covered in [errors "
"and optionality](errors.md) and [the reference on "
"opaque](../reference/opaque.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:343
msgid "Bounds connect interfaces to generics"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:345
msgid ""
"`fn name<T requires Labeled>(x: T)` admits exactly the instantiations whose "
"concrete type satisfies `Labeled` — the same member-set check, run where `T` "
"is chosen — see [functions, closures, and generics](functions.md). The bound "
"is what makes interface use legal inside the body: `let w: Labeled = x;`."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:351
msgid "Making your type iterable"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:353
msgid ""
"A type becomes a `for..of` target by marking an inherent member `[iterable]` "
"— the bracket marker designates the member (the NAME is free; the element "
"type falls out of the member's own emit parameter). No import is needed — "
"the marker word IS the designation; the builtin sequences never mark one:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:359
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"class CountUp { n: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"impl CountUp {\n"
"    [iterable] pub fn iterate(self, emit: fn(i32) -> bool) {\n"
"        for (let i = 1; i <= self.n; i += 1) {\n"
"            if (!emit(i)) { return; }   // false = stop\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"iter\");\n"
"    let ups = CountUp { n: 4 };\n"
"    let got: Vec<i32> = Vec.new();   // shared: survives the loop's "
"captures\n"
"    for (let v of ups) {\n"
"        got.push(v);\n"
"    }\n"
"    log.info(f\"n={got.len()} first={got[0]} last={got[got.len()-1]}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:388
msgid ""
"`for (let v of it)` desugars to `it.iterate(emit)` with a synthetic closure, "
"and the capture law covers it like every closure: scalars copy (a rebind "
"inside the loop stays local), ref-headed bindings share their slot — "
"reassigning one inside the loop moves the original, and the loop variable is "
"ONE variable reassigned per iteration, exactly like a handwritten `while` "
"(see [functions, closures, and generics](functions.md)). Accumulating "
"through a shared `vec.push(v)` works, and so does plain reassignment."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:397
msgid ""
"The builtin sequences (`[T]`, `Vec<T>`, `str`, `bytes`) iterate without the "
"marker — their loops are fused, never a per-element call."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:402
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub interface Shape {\n"
"    fn area(self) -> f64;\n"
"    fn name(self) -> str;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class Circle { r: f64; }\n"
"class Square { side: f64; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Circle {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return 3.14159 * self.r * self.r; }\n"
"    pub fn name(self) -> str { return \"circle\"; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Square {\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return self.side * self.side; }\n"
"    pub fn name(self) -> str { return \"square\"; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn describe(s: Shape) -> str {\n"
"    return f\"{s.name()}={s.area()}\";\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"interfaces\");\n"
"\n"
"    let c: Shape = Circle { r: 1.0 };        // satisfaction assertion\n"
"    log.info(describe(c));                    // single origin: static call\n"
"\n"
"    let sq = Square { side: 2.0 };\n"
"    log.info(f\"is shape: {sq is Shape}\");\n"
"\n"
"    let mut shapes: Vec<Shape> = Vec.new();   // heterogeneous storage\n"
"    shapes.push(Circle { r: 1.0 });\n"
"    shapes.push(Square { side: 3.0 });\n"
"    let mut total: f64 = 0.0;\n"
"    for (let s of shapes) {\n"
"        total += s.area();                    // itable per element\n"
"    }\n"
"    log.info(f\"total={total} n={shapes.len()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/interfaces.md:454
msgid "Next: [errors and optionality](errors.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:3
msgid "rut has no exceptions you catch. Two mechanisms cover everything:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:6
msgid "**Absence** is `nil` on a nullable `?T`."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:7
msgid ""
"**Failure** is a value in the second slot of a pair — the `(T, err)` answer "
"channel."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:9
msgid ""
"**Bugs** — broken contracts — trap loudly and immediately (`panic`, a nil "
"deref, an out-of-bounds index)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:12
msgid ""
"The design background lives in [everything is a "
"value](../core-concepts/everything-is-a-value.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:15
msgid "`?T` and `nil`: absence"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:17
msgid ""
"`?T` is a nil-able cell. A plain `T` always holds a value; `?T` is that "
"value or `nil`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:20
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Node {\n"
"    value: i32;\n"
"    left: ?Node;      // one word — recursive shapes are legal\n"
"    right: ?Node;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"nodes\");\n"
"    let n = Node { value: 1, left: nil, right: nil };\n"
"    log.info(f\"left is nil: {n.left == nil}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:40
msgid ""
"Lookups answer `?T`: a hit is the value, a miss is `nil`. Deref is automatic "
"at every use — `p.x`, `p[i]`, `for (let x of p)`, arithmetic — and "
"dereferencing a `nil` **traps** `NilDeref`, so guard first:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:44
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use nmapset::{ HashMap };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"optional\");\n"
"    let mut scores: HashMap<str, i32> = HashMap.new();\n"
"    scores.put(\"rut\", 41);\n"
"    let mut n = 0;\n"
"    let hit = scores.get(\"rut\");      // ?i32\n"
"    if (hit != nil) {\n"
"        n = hit + 1;                  // a hit auto-unwraps for `+`\n"
"    }\n"
"    log.info(f\"n={n}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:65
msgid ""
"`p == nil` compares against the null. There is no flow-typing: after the "
"guard, hand the value to a typed binding when you want a plain `T`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:68
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"let err = map_response(resp, \"no such repo or ref\", "
"\"data.jsdelivr.com\");\n"
"if (err != nil) {\n"
"    let why: str = err;           // the plain str behind the nullable\n"
"    eprint(why);\n"
"    return 1;\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:77
msgid ""
"`nil` in an expected-`?T` position just works: `let p: ?Node = nil;`, or a "
"`left: nil` field in a literal."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:80
msgid "The `(T, err)` answer channel"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:82
msgid "Functions that can fail return a pair. The convention is uniform:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:84
msgid "**empty err + a value** — success;"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:85
msgid "**empty err + `nil`** — a legitimate \"not found\";"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:86
msgid "**non-empty err** — failure."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:88
msgid "The simplest err is a `str` message (empty string means success):"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:90
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"fn hex_dec(s: str) -> (bytes, str) {\n"
"    if (s.len() % 2 != 0) {\n"
"        return (bytes.zeroed(0), \"hex: odd-length input\");\n"
"    }\n"
"    // ... decode ...\n"
"    return (out.freeze(), \"\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:100
msgid ""
"Nullable halves make both slots explicit — this is the standard library's "
"own shape, `(?T, ?E)`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:103
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"// json's entry points\n"
"fn decodeJson<T requires JsonDeserialize>(s: str) -> (?T, "
"?DecodeJsonError);\n"
"fn encodeJson<T requires JsonSerialize>(v: T) -> (?str, ?EncodeJsonError);\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:109
msgid "The caller destructures and checks the err half first:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:111
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use json::{ decodeJson, JsonI64 };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"json\");\n"
"    let (n, e) = decodeJson<JsonI64>(\"42\");\n"
"    if (e == nil) {\n"
"        log.info(f\"n={n.get()}\");\n"
"    } else {\n"
"        let why = e;\n"
"        log.info(f\"decode failed at {why.at}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:131
msgid ""
"Integer arithmetic's checked ladder uses the same idea in miniature: "
"`x.checked_add(y)` answers `(T, bool)` where `.1` is `false` exactly when "
"the result escaped the width — see [values and "
"variables](values-and-variables.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:136
msgid "Building an error type"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:138
msgid ""
"For errors a caller might want to _branch on_, pair a payloadless enum (the "
"kind) with a fixed-field struct (the details). That is the shape `json` uses:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:142
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"pub enum DecodeErrorKind { Unexpected, Truncated, InvalidUtf8, WrongType, "
"Depth, Trailing }\n"
"\n"
"pub struct DecodeJsonError {\n"
"    kind: DecodeErrorKind;\n"
"    at: i64;        // codepoint offset in the input\n"
"    got: str;       // the offending text\n"
"    expected: str;  // what the schema asked for here\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:153
msgid "The caller picks: branch on `kind`, or just render the fields:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:155
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use json::{ decodeJson, JsonI64 };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"json\");\n"
"    let (bad, be) = decodeJson<JsonI64>(\"[1,2,3]\");\n"
"    if (be != nil) {\n"
"        let why = be;\n"
"        // rejected at 0: got '[', wanted an i64\n"
"        log.info(f\"rejected at {why.at}: got '{why.got}', wanted "
"{why.expected}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:174
msgid ""
"Enums have no payloads — the struct carries the data. This split keeps every "
"error a plain value: copy it, store it in a log, put a location next to it, "
"test it."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:178
msgid "`panic`: for bugs, not for flow"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:180
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"panic(\"Rect: negative extents\");        // abort with a message\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:184
msgid ""
"Use it when continuing would be a lie: a broken invariant, an impossible "
"state, a caller error that is a programming mistake. Data problems — a bad "
"byte in a file, a missing key, a network error — are _values_ and belong in "
"the answer channel, so callers can recover."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:189
msgid ""
"There is no builtin `assert`: if a package wants one, it writes the two-line "
"helper over `panic` and owns it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:192
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"fn assert(c: bool, m: str) { if (!c) { panic(m); } }\n"
"\n"
"assert(total == expected, \"checksum\");  // abort when the condition is "
"false\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:198
msgid ""
"A trap's message and a stack trace (opt-in via `capture_stacktrace()`) land "
"in the host's diagnostics — see [diagnostics, traces, and "
"symbolication](../reference/diagnostics.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:202
msgid "Erasure when you truly need it"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:204
msgid ""
"When a value must cross a boundary that cannot name its type — host handles, "
"heterogeneous boxes — `opaque(v)` seals it and `opaque.downcast<T>(o) -> ?T` "
"recovers it (`nil` on a wrong type, never a trap):"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:209
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32 }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"opaque\");\n"
"    let box1 = opaque(Point { x: 1, y: 2 });\n"
"    let p = opaque.downcast<Point>(box1);   // ?Point\n"
"    when (p != nil) {\n"
"        true -> { log.info(f\"point {p.x} {p.y}\"); },\n"
"        else -> { log.info(\"point: nil\"); },\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:229
msgid ""
"`x is T` probes the box without recovering it. This is rut's only "
"`any`\\-shaped value, and it can do nothing until recovered — see [the "
"reference on opaque](../reference/opaque.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:235
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use json::{ decodeJson, encodeJson, JsonArr, JsonI64 };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"json\");\n"
"\n"
"    // a successful decode: (value, nil)\n"
"    let (n, e) = decodeJson<JsonI64>(\"42\");\n"
"    if (e == nil) {\n"
"        log.info(f\"n={n.get()}\");\n"
"    } else {\n"
"        let why = e;\n"
"        log.info(f\"decode failed at {why.at}: {why.expected}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    // encode answers the same shape: (?str, ?EncodeJsonError) —\n"
"    // through the spelled wrapper: a bare [i64] never satisfies\n"
"    let (s, ee) = encodeJson(JsonArr([JsonI64(1), JsonI64(2), "
"JsonI64(3)]));\n"
"    if (ee == nil) {\n"
"        let text = s;\n"
"        log.info(f\"s={text}\");\n"
"    } else {\n"
"        log.info(\"encode failed\");\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    // a bad decode: the (nil, err) half answers the failure\n"
"    let (bad, be) = decodeJson<JsonI64>(\"[1,2,3]\");\n"
"    if (be == nil) {\n"
"        log.info(\"unexpected success\");\n"
"    } else {\n"
"        let why = be;\n"
"        log.info(f\"rejected at {why.at}: got '{why.got}', wanted "
"{why.expected}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/errors.md:278
msgid "Next: [modules and packages](modules.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/modules.md:3
msgid ""
"A rut file is a module: one namespace, one visibility scope. A directory "
"with a `rut.jsonc` is a _package_ — the unit you depend on and share. This "
"chapter walks up those two levels. The reference pages are [modules and "
"visibility](../reference/modules-and-visibility.md), [project structure and "
"rut.jsonc](../reference/project-structure.md), and [dependency "
"kinds](../reference/dependency-kinds.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/modules.md:10
msgid "What lives at module scope"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/modules.md:12
msgid ""
"Module scope contains **declarations only**: `use`, `let`, `fn`, `struct`, "
"`class`, `enum`, `interface`, `impl`. Every statement lives inside a "
"function — and **loading a module executes nothing**. There is no load-time "
"side-effect ordering to reason about; the host loads your module and calls "
"one of its `entry fn`s (`entry fn main` for a plain program)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/modules.md:19
msgid ""
"Module-level `let` initializers must be load-time literals — `42`, "
"`\"app\"`, `true`. Arithmetic, record literals, and calls to user functions "
"are not accepted in this build (there is no mutable module state; programs "
"build their state in `main`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/modules.md:24
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: f32; y: f32 }\n"
"\n"
"let version = 1;                       // fine: a literal\n"
"let app_name = \"app\";                  // any literal works\n"
"\n"
"fn main_body() { /* statements live here */ }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"app\");\n"
"    let origin = Point { x: 0, y: 0 }; // record literals live in function "
"bodies\n"
"    log.info(f\"{app_name} v{version} origin.x={origin.x}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/modules.md:46 src/reference/modules-and-visibility.md:107
msgid "Visibility"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/modules.md:48
msgid ""
"Every declaration has a visibility, and unannotated means **module-private** "
"— the safe default. Nothing leaks unless it says `pub`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorial/modules.md:52 src/reference/modules-and-visibility.md:113
#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:111
msgid "Form"
msgstr ""

