msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:107
msgid ""
"`Vec<u8>` is the mutable binary builder; `bytes` is the binary type that "
"crosses the host boundary (see [Primitive types](primitive-types.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:111
msgid "`Weak<T>` — the weak reference"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:113
msgid ""
"`Weak<T>` is a builtin class whose box holds an _unretained_ word to a "
"referent — a weak never keeps anything alive. The name is **import-gated**: "
"spell `use core::{ Weak };` or the bare `Weak` diagnoses `` `Weak` is not in "
"scope — `use core::{ Weak }` ``."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:118
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ Weak };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Tile { v: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let tile = Tile { v: 7 };\n"
"    let w = Weak.new(tile);        // the class-method construction\n"
"    let got: ?Tile = w.upgrade();  // the live referent, or nil once dead\n"
"    log.info(f\"{got.v}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:137
msgid ""
"`Weak.new(v)` traps on a `nil` `v`; `T` must be a reference type "
"(`Weak<i32>` diagnoses — primitives move by value). `Weak<?U>` is legal and "
"`upgrade()` answers `??U`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:140
msgid ""
"The referent's death nulls every weak box before any user code runs; "
"`upgrade()` answers `nil` deterministically from then on."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:142
msgid ""
"A weak edge closes no cycle: strong cycles still leak — see [Rc, dispose, "
"and identity](rc-dispose-identity.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:145
msgid "Keyed collections"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:147
msgid ""
"`HashMap<K, V>` and `HashSet<T>` (package `nmapset`) wrap a native key "
"table. Admission is the compile-time **union bound** `K requires i8 | i16 | "
"i32 | i64 | u8 | u16 | u32 | u64 | bool | str | bytes` — a key type outside "
"the set fails at the instantiation (escape hatch: encode it canonically to "
"`bytes`). Float keys are absent by design: floats have no stable equality "
"contract. The API is `new`/`with_capacity`/`put`/`get`/`has`/`remove`/`len`; "
"`get` answers `?V` — `nil` is absent, and a hit returns the **stored cell**, "
"not a copy (the aliasing law)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:157
msgid "Absence and errors"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:159
msgid ""
"**Absence** is `nil` on a nullable: a lookup returns `?V`, and `nil` means "
"\"not found\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:161
msgid ""
"**Errors** are the answer channel: `(?T, err)` — see [Primitive "
"types](primitive-types.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:163
msgid ""
"**Type-erased recovery** is `opaque.downcast<T>(o) -> ?T` — see [opaque — "
"erasure and downcast](opaque.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:166
msgid ""
"A nullable/enum value is not compared structurally with `==` — test it with "
"`when`, a `nil`/`!= nil` guard, or the payload."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:3
msgid ""
"`enum` — simple named integer sets. This is the entire feature: rut has **no "
"data-carrying enums**. Heterogeneous data goes through interfaces (see "
"[Interfaces and dispatch](interfaces.md)); absence goes through `?T` (see "
"[Builtin generic types](builtin-generic-types.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:8
msgid "Syntax"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:15
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }              // 0, 1, 2\n"
"enum Direction { Up = 1, Down, Left, Right } // 1, 2, 3, 4\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"{Color.Blue} {Direction.Right}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:31
msgid ""
"An enum is a distinct named type over fixed-width integer constants. Members "
"are the enum's values: implicit numbering continues from the last value "
"(starting at 0); an explicit initializer (a possibly negative integer "
"literal) resets the counter."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:35
msgid ""
"No data payloads and no computed members — ever. Methods are an `impl` block "
"away (see [Impl blocks](#impl-blocks))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:38
msgid "Using members"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:40
msgid "Members are named through the enum and compare as equal singletons:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:42
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Light { Red, Yellow, Green }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let l = Light.Yellow;\n"
"    log.info(f\"{l == Light.Yellow}\");   // true — members are immortal "
"singleton cells\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:58
msgid ""
"An enum value renders as its member name in format strings: "
"`f\"{Color.Red}\"` is `\"Red\"`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:60
msgid "Enums are ordinary shared values: binding shares the singleton."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:62
msgid "Exhaustive `when`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:64
msgid ""
"`when` over an enum must be **exhaustive**: cover every member (then `else` "
"is optional) or add an explicit `else` arm — a partial `when` is a compile "
"error. See [Control flow and when](control-flow.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:68
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Light { Green, Yellow, Red }\n"
"\n"
"fn go() { let log = Logger.new(\"t\"); log.info(\"go\"); }\n"
"fn brake() { let log = Logger.new(\"t\"); log.info(\"brake\"); }\n"
"fn stop() { let log = Logger.new(\"t\"); log.info(\"stop\"); }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let l = Light.Yellow;\n"
"    when (l) {\n"
"        Light.Green  -> { go(); },\n"
"        Light.Yellow -> { brake(); },\n"
"        Light.Red    -> { stop(); },   // all members: no else needed\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:91
msgid "Impl blocks"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:93
msgid ""
"Enums take `impl` blocks — the same two forms a struct or class takes: "
"non-self methods (called on the enum's name) and `self` methods (called on a "
"value). A marked `[iterable]` member makes the enum a `for..of` target."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:98
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Dial { Low, High }\n"
"\n"
"impl Dial {\n"
"    fn default() -> Self { return Dial.Low; }\n"
"    fn flipped(self) -> Dial {\n"
"        return when (self) {\n"
"            Dial.Low  -> Dial.High,\n"
"            Dial.High -> Dial.Low,\n"
"        };\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let d = Dial.default().flipped();\n"
"    log.info(when (d) {\n"
"        Dial.Low  -> \"low\",\n"
"        Dial.High -> \"high\",\n"
"    });\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:127
msgid ""
"A non-self method is a namespaced function: `Dial.default()`. `Self` spells "
"the enum, so `-> Self` is the return type and `Dial.Low` mints the member."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:130
msgid ""
"A `self` method dispatches on the value: `d.flipped()` — the receiver "
"crosses as the singleton cell it already is."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:132
msgid ""
"Method visibility is real: `pub fn` exports cross-module, plain `fn` is "
"module-private (see [Modules and visibility](modules-and-visibility.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:136
msgid ""
"A marked member makes enum values iterable — `for (let v of c)` rides the "
"same desugar as a class's (see [The iteration protocol](interfaces.md)):"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:140
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Light { Green, Yellow, Red }\n"
"\n"
"impl Light {\n"
"    [iterable] fn iterate(self, emit: fn(Light) -> bool) {\n"
"        let mut cur = self;\n"
"        for (let i = 0; i < 3; i += 1) {\n"
"            if (!emit(cur)) { return; }\n"
"            cur = when (cur) {\n"
"                Light.Green  -> Light.Yellow,\n"
"                Light.Yellow -> Light.Red,\n"
"                Light.Red    -> Light.Green,\n"
"            };\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    for (let l of Light.Red) {\n"
"        log.info(f\"{l}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:173
msgid ""
"One limit stays: `[disposal]` is for structs and classes only — the engine "
"disposes a record's cell, and an enum member is immortal."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:176
msgid "Boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:178
msgid ""
"Where another language would use a union of literals (`\"left\" | "
"\"right\"`), rut uses an enum; where it would use a union of _shapes_, rut "
"uses an interface-typed value (see [Interfaces and dispatch](interfaces.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:182
msgid ""
"The `|` spelling exists only for bound-only union aliases and `requires` "
"bounds (see [Type aliases and union bounds](type-aliases.md)) — a "
"compile-time admission gate, never a runtime union value."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/enums.md:185
msgid ""
"There is no enum↔int cast: `as` is the numeric cast only. Enum values cross "
"the host boundary as their runtime identity plus the integer value."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:3
msgid ""
"Numeric suffixes, the default-width rule, casts, tuple literals, string "
"literal forms, and the format-string desugaring."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:6
msgid "Inference and conversions"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:8
msgid ""
"Inference is bidirectional (literal ↔ expected type). A literal without "
"context defaults to `i32` (integers) / `f32` (floats) — and **the default is "
"also a ceiling**: an unsuffixed literal adapts to the expected type only "
"while it fits that default."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:13
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let a = 10;                // i32 (default)\n"
"    let b = 10u8;              // u8 via suffix\n"
"    let c: u64 = 10;           // u64 via annotation — fits the default\n"
"    let big = 18446744073709551615u64;  // past the i32 default: suffix "
"REQUIRED\n"
"    // let bad = 13503953896175478587;  // ERROR — exceeds i32, add `u64`\n"
"    log.info(f\"{a} {b} {c} {big}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:31
msgid ""
"Past the ceiling the literal must declare itself with a suffix, so a dropped "
"or doubled digit cannot silently re-base a constant. Floats: magnitude is "
"the trigger, precision is not — `0.1` is fine anywhere; `1.0e300` needs "
"`f64` even in an `f64` position."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:36
msgid ""
"Numeric literals: decimal and `0x` / `0b` / `0o`, `_` separators, suffixes "
"`u8..u64 i8..i64 f32 f64` (`3.14159f32`, `0xFF_u32`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:39
msgid "The cast — `expr as T`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:41
msgid "Conversions are casts, truncating like C/Rust:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:43
msgid "int→int keeps the target's low bits (signed targets sign-extend);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:44
msgid ""
"float→int truncates toward zero and saturates at the target bounds (`NaN` → "
"`0`);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:46
msgid "a conversion never traps (arithmetic still does)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:48
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use calc::{ Math };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let cast = 300 as u8;      // 44\n"
"    let x = 3;\n"
"    let y = 4;\n"
"    log.info(f\"{cast} {Math.sqrt((x * x + y * y) as f64)}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:65
msgid ""
"`as` binds tighter than `*`, is left-associative (`x as u32 as u64` chains), "
"and its right-hand side is a naming position restricted to the numeric "
"primitives. There are no implicit numeric conversions at all."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:69
msgid "Fixed arrays and repeats"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:71
msgid ""
"`[e1, .., en]` has type `[T]` — pure data whose literal allocates the array "
"cell; binding the value shares the cell. It infers `T` bidirectionally like "
"any literal; at module scope it is a load-time expression when every element "
"is (see [Modules and visibility](modules-and-visibility.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:76
msgid ""
"`[v; n]` is the repeat: `n` slots of the **value** `v` — see [Builtin "
"generic types](builtin-generic-types.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:81
msgid ""
"Tuples are first-class values: type `(T0, T1, ..)`, value `(a, b, ..)`, "
"numeric fields `.0` / `.1` / .., and destructuring:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:84
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn divmod(a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, i32) {\n"
"    return (a / b, a % b);\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let pair = (1, \"two\");         // (i32, str)\n"
"    let (n, s) = pair;\n"
"    log.info(f\"{n} {s} {divmod(7, 2).0}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:103
msgid ""
"The `(value, ok)` / `(T, err)` pair is the language's answer channel — see "
"[Primitive types](primitive-types.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:106
msgid "String literals"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:108
msgid ""
"Three forms — a plain `\"...\"` string is always inert (no interpolation "
"ever happens implicitly):"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:111 src/reference/control-flow.md:69
msgid "Example"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:113
msgid "plain"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:113
msgid "`\"hi\\tname\"`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:113
msgid "escapes processed: `\\t \\n \\r \\b \\f \\\\ \\\" \\u{...}`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:114
msgid "raw"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:114
msgid "`r\"C:\\temp\\log.txt\"`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:114
msgid "**no** escape processing; every byte is literal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:115
msgid "format"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:115
msgid "`f\"hi {name}, n={n}\"`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:115
msgid "Rust-style placeholders, evaluated at runtime"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:117
msgid "Raw and format do not combine (`rf\"...\"` does not parse)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:119
msgid "Format literals — `f\"...\"`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:121
msgid ""
"`{ expr }` splices an expression's value: identifiers, paths, calls, "
"arithmetic — any expression _except_ nested string literals (bind one to a "
"name first). The lexer balances braces to find the closing `}`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:124
msgid ""
"`{{` and `}}` are literal braces. Escapes work exactly like plain strings."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:126
msgid ""
"The literal desugars at compile time to a concatenation of the literal "
"chunks and one `str(x)` conversion per placeholder — a builtin per-type "
"formatting call, no runtime parsing:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:130
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"f\"a={a} b={f(b())}\"   ->   concat(\"a=\", str(a), \" b=\", str(f(b())))\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:134
msgid "Formattable types and their rendering:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:136
msgid "Rendering"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:138
msgid "ints"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:138
msgid "decimal, `-` for negatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:139
msgid "`f32`/`f64`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:139
msgid "shortest round-trip decimal (`3.5`, `0.1`, `1e300`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:141
msgid "contents, verbatim"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:142
msgid "enum"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:142
msgid "member name (`Color.Red` → `\"Red\"`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:144
msgid ""
"Everything else — struct/class values, vecs and fixed arrays, `?T` boxes, "
"`opaque`, tuples — is a **compile error** inside `f\"...\"` (preventing "
"accidental implementation-detail printing). Write a `to_string() -> str` "
"method on your type and call it explicitly, and route developer output "
"through your host logger (for example the `ink` package's "
"`Logger.debug(f\"...\")`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/literals-and-inference.md:151
msgid ""
"The accumulator idiom `out = f\"{out}{chunk}\"` is recognized by the "
"compiler and appends in place — amortized O(1) instead of copying the whole "
"prefix per step, linear in the total output. Core ships no string-builder "
"class: when a reusable builder reads better (a tokenizer or encoder "
"threading text through many calls), reach for the `strbuild` package's "
"`StringBuilder` ([stdlib](stdlib.md)); when an accumulator binding reads "
"better, the f-string shape IS the optimized path."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:3
msgid ""
"The structured statements, the indexed and iterating `for` forms, and `when` "
"— the one match construct, an exhaustive pattern _expression_. Branching on "
"a value is `when`: arms are `pattern -> body`, and the construct is "
"exhaustive."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:8
msgid "Statements"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:10
msgid "`if (cond) { .. } else if (..) { .. } else { .. }` — braces required."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:11
msgid "`while (cond) { .. }`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:12
msgid "`return expr?;` — `expr` is required unless the function returns `nil`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:14
msgid ""
"`break` / `continue` exist for loops; there are no labels and no `do..while`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:17
msgid "`for` — two forms"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:19
msgid ""
"Iterating form — vecs, fixed arrays, slices, strings (one-codepoint `str`s "
"per step), `bytes` (`u8` per step), and any type carrying an "
"`[iterable]`\\-marked member — an enum value included (see [Interfaces and "
"dispatch](interfaces.md) and [Enums](enums.md)). Both spellings of this form "
"are one loop: the loop variable is a single binding reassigned per "
"iteration, and over a marked type the loop calls that ONE designated member "
"with an `emit` closure that follows the capture law exactly like the fused "
"form:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:32
msgid ""
"Indexed form — the induction variable is **loop-owned**: the update clause "
"and the body may assign it without `mut`; it is not a normal binding:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:42
msgid ""
"`when (x) { pattern -> body, ... }` is an **expression**: the first matching "
"arm's body produces its value. No fallthrough; exactly one arm runs."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:46
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn describe(n: i32) -> str {\n"
"    return when (n) {\n"
"        0         -> \"zero\",\n"
"        1, 2, 3   -> \"small\",   // comma-separated alternatives\n"
"        else      -> \"big\",     // non-enum scrutinee: else REQUIRED\n"
"    };\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"{describe(0)} {describe(2)} {describe(10)}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:69
msgid "Pattern"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:71
msgid "enum member (dotted path)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:71
msgid "`Light.Green`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:72
msgid "literal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:72
msgid "`0`, `3.5`, `true`, `\"text\"`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:73
msgid "negative literal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:73
msgid "`-1`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:74
msgid "alternatives (comma list)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:74
msgid "`1, 2, 3`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:75
msgid "wildcard `else` / `_`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:75
msgid "`else -> ..`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:77
msgid ""
"Ranges, destructuring, binding patterns, and guards (`x if cond`) do not "
"exist; patterns are enum members, literals, alternatives, and the wildcards "
"only."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:81 src/reference/opaque.md:44
msgid "The laws"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:83
msgid ""
"**Exhaustiveness is always enforced.** An enum-typed scrutinee must cover "
"every member (then `else` is optional) or add `else` — a partial `when` is a "
"compile error. Any other scrutinee type (integers can't be enumerated): "
"`else` is **mandatory**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:87
msgid ""
"**All arms must agree on one type** — that is the `when`'s type. Used as a "
"statement, that type must be `nil` (arm bodies that are evaluated for effect "
"are written as block arms)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:90
msgid ""
"Duplicate patterns, and arms made unreachable by earlier ones, are compile "
"errors."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:92
msgid ""
"Arms use `->`. Expression arms are comma-separated; block arms may omit the "
"trailing comma (the corpus writes it)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:94
msgid "`when` literal patterns match compile-time values, never runtime `==`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:96
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"enum Light { Green, Yellow, Red }\n"
"\n"
"fn go() { let log = Logger.new(\"t\"); log.info(\"go\"); }\n"
"fn brake() { let log = Logger.new(\"t\"); log.info(\"brake\"); }\n"
"fn stop() { let log = Logger.new(\"t\"); log.info(\"stop\"); }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let l = Light.Yellow;\n"
"    when (l) {\n"
"        Light.Green  -> { go(); },\n"
"        Light.Yellow -> { brake(); },\n"
"        Light.Red    -> { stop(); },   // every member: else optional\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:119
msgid "A `when` is also the idiomatic nullable guard, together with `!= nil`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/control-flow.md:121
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let p: ?Point = Point { x: 7, y: 0 };\n"
"    when (p != nil) {\n"
"        true -> { log.info(f\"{p.x}\"); },\n"
"        else -> { /* absent */ },\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:3
msgid ""
"`struct` — the open data record: all fields public, constructed only by "
"literal, sharing its cell like every non-primitive."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:8
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point {\n"
"    x: f32;\n"
"    y: f32;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"struct Style {\n"
"    color: u32 = 0xff00ff;   // field initializer: literals may omit it\n"
"    width: f32 = 1;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let s = Style {};\n"
"    log.info(f\"{s.color} {s.width}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:32 src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:6
msgid "Reference semantics"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:34
msgid ""
"A struct value is a **heap cell handle** (see [By-reference and "
"nullable](by-reference-and-nullable.md)): assignment, argument passing, and "
"returning share the cell, and a mutation through any alias is visible "
"through all of them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:39
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let mut p = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n"
"    let q = p;                       // SHARE: q and p name one cell (O(1))\n"
"    p.x = 4;                         // q.x is 4 now — sharing is the law\n"
"    log.info(f\"{q.x}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:57
msgid ""
"Writing is gated by the `mut`\\-binding law (see [Modules and "
"visibility](modules-and-visibility.md)): field stores and `mut self` methods "
"need a `let mut` binding (or a `mut` parameter). Parameters declare their "
"intent: `fn nudge(mut pt: Point)` may write the caller's point; `fn "
"length(pt: Point) -> f64` promises not to, and returns a fresh record "
"instead:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:64
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"fn nudged(pt: Point) -> Point {     // builds a NEW record\n"
"    return Point { x: pt.x + 1, y: pt.y };\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let p2 = nudged(Point { x: 1, y: 2 });\n"
"    log.info(f\"{p2.x} {p2.y}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:84
msgid ""
"`==` on two struct values is a **cell-identity test** — `q == p` is true "
"exactly when they name one cell; two separately built literals are never "
"equal. Field-wise comparison is an interface-shaped contract of your own "
"(spell a member that compares — see [Interfaces and "
"dispatch](interfaces.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:90
msgid "Construction — the literal, everywhere"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:92
msgid ""
"`Name { field: expr, .. }` is the **only** construction. There is no `new`, "
"no class methods, no type-call. The literal is available everywhere — "
"function bodies and module-level `let` initializers alike — and it allocates "
"the cell."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:97
msgid "Fields may be given in any order, by name."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:98
msgid "The literal must initialize **every** field that has no initializer."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:99
msgid ""
"An omitted field with an initializer takes it; `{}` with all-default fields "
"is legal."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:102
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }\n"
"struct Style { color: u32 = 0xff00ff; width: f32 = 1; }\n"
"struct Rect { min: Point; max: Point; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let q = Point { x: 9, y: 9 };\n"
"    let s = Style {};                     // zero-value defaults fill the "
"fields\n"
"    let r = Rect { min: Point { x: 0, y: 0 }, max: q };  // max shares q's "
"cell\n"
"    log.info(f\"{s.color} {r.max.x}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:122
msgid "All fields public, always"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:124
msgid ""
"A struct is an open data record: member visibility in a struct body is a "
"compile error (privacy needs construction control, which is the class's job "
"— see [Classes and constructors](classes.md)). Structs also have no `static` "
"members."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:129
msgid "Methods live in impl blocks"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:131
msgid ""
"A struct body is **fields only** — a `fn` member in the body is a hard parse "
"error. Methods live in the inherent `impl S { .. }`, in the type's module "
"only — the one impl form (see [Interfaces and dispatch](interfaces.md)):"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:136
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"impl Point {\n"
"    fn dist(self, other: Point) -> f32 { .. }   // inherent — the type's "
"module\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Point {\n"
"    fn hash(self) -> u64 { .. }                 // a member set, if you want "
"one:\n"
"    fn eq(self, other: Point) -> bool { .. }    // satisfaction reads these\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:147
msgid ""
"Methods carry real visibility — the class rule: `pub fn` exports "
"cross-module, plain `fn` is module-private. `Self` spells the struct, in "
"signatures (`-> Self`) and the literal (`Self { x: 1, y: 2 }`) alike:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:152
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Counter { n: i32 }\n"
"\n"
"impl Counter {\n"
"    pub fn new() -> Self { return Counter { n: 0 }; }\n"
"    fn bump(mut self) -> Self { self.n += 1; return self; }\n"
"    fn value(self) -> i32 { return self.n; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let c = Counter.new().bump().bump();\n"
"    log.info(f\"{c.value()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:174
msgid ""
"Free functions over data remain the default idiom; methods are for tight "
"helpers, impl blocks for the members other code observes."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:177
msgid "Limits, exhaustively:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:179
msgid ""
"**fields are always public** — no field-visibility dial (privacy needs "
"construction control, which is the class's job — see [Classes and "
"constructors](classes.md));"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:182
msgid ""
"**construction is the literal** — `Point { x: 1, y: 2 }` everywhere (open "
"literal vs class-method-gated _is_ the struct/class distinction)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:186
msgid ""
"Everything else class-shaped is allowed — `impl` blocks, `Self`, "
"`[disposal]`. And the old \"no destructor\" limit is gone: a shared value "
"dies exactly when its cell's refcount reaches zero, so cleanup is one `impl` "
"away — mark a `[disposal]` member on the type and the engine calls `dispose` "
"at that moment (see [Rc, dispose, and identity](rc-dispose-identity.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:193
msgid "Interfaces and representation"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:195
msgid ""
"Widening a struct to an interface `I` **attaches the itable to the same "
"handle** — no allocation, no copy: the interface-typed value aliases the "
"record, and mutations through it are visible to every other handle."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:199
msgid ""
"Representation: one slot per field inside the cell, in declaration order — "
"primitive fields widened into their slot, composite fields as cell-handle "
"slots (see [Reified types and layout](reified-types.md)). Recursive shapes "
"(`next: ?Node`) are legal because composite fields are pointer-sized."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/structs.md:204
msgid ""
"Structs are for small data (points, rects, colors, configs), but any size is "
"allowed."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:3
msgid ""
"`class` — the sealed record: module-private fields by default, construction "
"gated behind class methods, no inheritance."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:6
msgid "Declaration and construction"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:8
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"class Rect {\n"
"    w: f32;\n"
"    h: f32;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Rect {\n"
"    pub fn new(w: f32, h: f32) -> Self {   // construction VALIDATES — it "
"is\n"
"        if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) {            // just a function\n"
"            panic(\"Rect: negative extents\");\n"
"        }\n"
"        return Self { w: w, h: h };\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn from_square(s: f32) -> Self {   // named constructors are "
"siblings\n"
"        return Rect.new(s, s);\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    pub fn area(self) -> f32 { return self.w * self.h; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let r = Rect.from_square(3);\n"
"    log.info(f\"area={r.area()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:42
msgid ""
"**Classes construct through their own class methods — nothing else is "
"constructible.** No outside literal exists:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:45
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"let r = Rect.new(3, 4);            // the one construction surface\n"
"let v = Version.parse(\"1.2\");      // ?Version — nil on failure\n"
"// Rect { w: 1, h: 1 };            // ERROR: classes have no outside "
"literal\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:51
msgid ""
"`new` is not special syntax — just the conventional primary-constructor name "
"(`from`, `parse`, `open`, `default` are its siblings); it is an ordinary "
"identifier. Try-construction returns the nullable: a class method `fn "
"parse(s: str) -> ?Version` answers `nil` on failure."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:56
msgid ""
"**The `Self { field: expr, .. }` literal is the class-private construction** "
"— legal anywhere inside the class body (class methods and instance methods "
"alike); the class name spells it inside the body too (`Rect { .. }`). The "
"literal must initialize every field without an initializer; field "
"initializers run for omitted fields. Private fields are settable in the "
"literal — inside the class body only. That privacy _is_ the seal: outside "
"code can build a class value only by calling a class method that chooses to "
"build one."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:65
msgid ""
"**No implicit default construction.** A class whose fields all have "
"initializers still needs an explicit `fn new() -> Self { return Self {}; }` "
"if outsiders should build it. A class with no accessible constructing class "
"method is **sealed** — constructible only inside its own body."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:71
msgid ""
"**No parameter properties**: parameters are parameters — the `Self { field: "
"name }` literal makes the param→field mapping explicit."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:74
msgid ""
"**`async` class methods are allowed** — same function, `await` in the body. "
"No partially constructed instance ever exists across an `await`: the `Self { "
".. }` literal is an ordinary expression, and locals live in the coroutine "
"frame."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:79
msgid "Newtypes: the one-field wrapper"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:81
msgid ""
"`class Name(Wrapped);` — the positional one-field decl. It IS an ordinary "
"class: exactly one field, `inner`, of the wrapped type. The positional "
"spelling additionally provides the constructor — the call form `Name(value)`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:86
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"class JsonI64(i64);              // the wrapper family — one per wrapped\n"
"class JsonI32(i32);              // type, each with its own body\n"
"class JsonF64(f64);\n"
"impl JsonI64 {\n"
"    pub fn encode(mut self, w: JsonWriter) -> nil { w.int(self.inner); }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"dump(JsonI64(64));               // manufacture at the boundary\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:97
msgid ""
"**The wrapped type is unrestricted** — primitives, composites, tuples, fn "
"types, another module's class: `class Opt(?i64);`, `class Pair((i32, "
"str));`, `class Cb(fn(i32) -> str);` are all legal. The wrapped position is "
"a FIELD type, never an impl target — that is the point. A wrapper is how a "
"primitive or a third-party type gains a capability satisfaction can't reach: "
"declare the wrapper, implement on it, hand the wrapper across the boundary."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:104
msgid ""
"**No auto-insertion, ever.** `dump(64)` is an error forever; the capability "
"exists exactly where the constructor is spelled. And no implicit forwarding: "
"the wrapper exposes exactly its own members — `inner` (the ordinary field, "
"ordinary visibility) and its `impl` methods."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:109
msgid ""
"**The naming law**: wrappers are named for what they wrap (`JsonI64`, never "
"`JsonExt`). Per-type wrappers with type-specific bodies when the bodies "
"differ; a generic wrapper covers the uniform cases: `class DebugWrap<T>(T);`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:113
msgid ""
"**The generic instantiation law**: a binder is determined by the wrapped "
"argument when it appears there (`class Tail<T>([T]);` → `Tail([1, 2])` is a "
"`Tail<i32>`), or by explicit type arguments at the constructor "
"(`Converter<i32>(\"64\")` — `class Converter<T>(str);` cannot infer, the "
"binder is not in `str`). If any binder is undetermined, the call spells ALL "
"binders — all-or-nothing: `class Pair<A, B>(i32);` accepts `Pair<i32, "
"str>(7)`, never `Pair<i32>(7)`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:121
msgid ""
"**The third-party adapter** (the orphan case, dissolved): `class "
"TheirJson(TheirType);` plus an inherent `encode` hands THEIR type the "
"capability — consumers manufacture `TheirJson(x)` from their own modules; "
"the newtype flag rides the class's surface row like the class itself does."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:126
msgid ""
"A braced class — even one with a single `inner` field — has no call "
"construction. The positional spelling is what provides the constructor; "
"everything else stays sealed."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:130
msgid ""
"At runtime the construction mints a real cell: the wrapper is a value like "
"every class."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:133
msgid "Methods: explicit `self`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:135
msgid ""
"**The receiver is explicit.** An instance method spells its receiver as the "
"first parameter — `fn add(self, x: i32, y: i32)` — and the body reads fields "
"through `self`. A method that mutates declares `mut self` and requires a "
"`let mut` receiver (see [Modules and visibility](modules-and-visibility.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:140
msgid ""
"A method **without** a `self` parameter is a **class method** — invoked on "
"the class itself (`Rect.new(..)`, `Self.new(..)` inside the body). Presence "
"or absence of `self` is the whole distinction. Class methods are ordinary "
"functions: they validate, default, cache, register, or hand out singletons."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:145
msgid ""
"**A computed property is a method** (`c.count()`), and a settable one takes "
"an argument (`c.set_count(n)`). One member kind, one call convention."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:148
msgid ""
"**Static fields** are declared `static name: T = init;` in the class body, "
"with the same visibility forms as fields."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:151
msgid "Member visibility"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:153
msgid ""
"Members follow the same visibility forms as declarations (see [Modules and "
"visibility](modules-and-visibility.md)): an unannotated field or method is "
"**module-private**; `pub`, `pub(mod)`, `pub(super)`, `pub(self)` expose it "
"to the form's audience. The construction surface is therefore explicit: `pub "
"fn new(..)` builds; unannotated members stay the class's own business within "
"its module."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:160
msgid "Reference semantics and equality"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:162
msgid ""
"A class value is a **heap cell handle** like every non-primitive (see "
"[By-reference and nullable](by-reference-and-nullable.md)): assignment "
"shares, mutation is visible through aliases. `==` is cell identity; "
"field-wise comparison is an interface you write for it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:167
msgid ""
"Reflection is opt-in: a class is walkable only where a serialization member "
"has been written for it by hand; structs are the open, auto-walkable records."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:171
msgid "No inheritance"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:173
msgid ""
"**No `extends` for classes** — no base-class constructors (`super(..)`), no "
"method overriding, no `super.m()`, no `protected`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:175
msgid ""
"Code sharing is composition (hold a helper object or struct in a field) or "
"free functions; polymorphism is only the structural kind — a type used "
"through an interface whose members it has (see [Interfaces and "
"dispatch](interfaces.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/classes.md:179
msgid ""
"Layout stays trivial: fields at fixed offsets — identical inside every cell "
"payload (see [Reified types and layout](reified-types.md)) — no prefix "
"layout, no fat pointers, and every object has exactly one concrete class "
"forever. That keeps `is` a single descriptor check."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:3
msgid ""
"One memory regime: every non-primitive value is a shared, refcounted heap "
"cell; destruction is deterministic; `==` is identity for cells."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:8
msgid ""
"Every non-primitive value — struct and class records, `str`, `bytes`, `Vec`, "
"`[T]`, enums, `opaque` boxes, interface-typed values, `?T` boxes, closures' "
"captured cells — is a **heap cell handle**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:12
msgid ""
"assignment, argument passing, and returning copy the handle "
"(retain/release), an O(1) move;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:14
msgid "**mutation is visible through every alias**;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:15
msgid ""
"writing is gated by the `mut`\\-binding law (see [Modules and "
"visibility](modules-and-visibility.md)), never by the sharing."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:19
msgid ""
"There is no eager copy of any composite — bindings share by reference. "
"**`bytes.clone()` is the one copy escape hatch**: a one-shot deep copy of a "
"buffer's octets. Every other type shares on binding; a divergent value of "
"any other type is unreachable — build a new one instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:24
msgid ""
"Recursive shapes (`next: ?Node`, trees, lists) are legal: composite fields "
"and elements are pointer-sized handle slots, and the refcount walk sees "
"every handle field via the compile-time field table (see [Reified types and "
"layout](reified-types.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:29
msgid "Disposal — the cell-death contract (the `[disposal]` marker)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:31
msgid ""
"Destructors are implemented, not attached. A type opts in with one "
"`[disposal]`\\-marked member on its inherent impl (the name is free — the "
"bracket designates):"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:35
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ DisposalContext };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Conn { log: Logger; url: str; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Conn {\n"
"    [disposal] fn close(mut self, cx: DisposalContext) {\n"
"        self.log.info(f\"closed {self.url}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let c = Conn { log: log, url: \"tcp://edge\" };\n"
"    log.info(\"main is done\");\n"
"}   // c's refcount reaches zero here; dispose runs at the call boundary\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:54
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"main is done\n"
"closed tcp://edge\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:59
msgid ""
"A `[disposal]` member runs when the cell's refcount reaches **zero** — "
"deterministic destruction, not a collector callback. The dying value arrives "
"as `mut self`; the fields release after the body returns. The member's NAME "
"IS FREE — the bracket designates, the spelling never does (one `[disposal]` "
"member per class)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:64
msgid ""
"`cx: DisposalContext` is **engine-minted**, one per call. It is empty today "
"— the parameter exists so the context can grow additively without ever "
"touching the marker's signature."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:67
msgid ""
"`DisposalContext` is the **import-gated** builtin class: `use core::{ "
"DisposalContext };` brings it in ([core and the swappable "
"packages](stdlib.md)). Using it without the use line diagnoses `` "
"`DisposalContext` is not in scope — `use core::{ DisposalContext }` ``. The "
"marker stands alone — there is no `Disposal` interface behind it and no "
"import for the bracket word itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:74
msgid ""
"One marked member per class, by the marker's own law — there is no per-value "
"attach and nothing to attach twice."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:76
msgid ""
"The body runs at a call boundary, not re-entrantly inside the release; the "
"full sequence — weak boxes nulled first, then `dispose`, then the recursive "
"field walk — is [the Rc heap](rc-heap.md)'s destruction order."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:80
msgid ""
"The host side of the same law: a host payload's finalizer runs at cell "
"death, before the payload's own Rust `Drop`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:83
msgid ""
"Because every shared value now has a single knowable death — its refcount "
"reaching zero — a struct can carry its own destructor: no wrapper class and "
"no mint-site bookkeeping, just the impl."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:89
msgid ""
"`Weak<T>` (see [Builtin generic types](builtin-generic-types.md)) demotes "
"any handle to a non-keeping reference:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:92
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ Weak };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Tile { v: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let tile = Tile { v: 7 };\n"
"    let w = Weak.new(tile);        // does NOT keep the cell alive\n"
"    let got: ?Tile = w.upgrade();  // the live referent, or nil once dead\n"
"    log.info(f\"{got.v}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:111
msgid ""
"The referent's death nulls every weak box before any user code runs. A weak "
"edge closes no cycle: strong cycles (two records holding each other, a "
"container that holds its own observer) still leak to engine shutdown — the "
"memory law is the program's; break cycles with `Weak`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:116
msgid "Identity — `==`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:118
msgid ""
"`a == b` is a builtin operator: no dispatch, no opting in, no element-wise "
"story. `a != b` is its negation."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:121
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:97
msgid "Operand type"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:121
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:97
msgid "Lowering"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:123
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:99
msgid "numeric / `bool` primitives"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:123
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:99
msgid "value"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:123
msgid "compare, IEEE 754 for floats (`NaN != NaN`, `-0.0 == 0.0`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:124
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:100
msgid "content (codepoints)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:124
#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:125
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:100
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:101
msgid "content compare"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:125
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:101
msgid "content (octets)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:126
msgid ""
"everything else — records, arrays, `Vec`, enums, closures, interface "
"objects, `opaque`, `?T`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:126
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:102
msgid "**cell identity**"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:126
#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:102
msgid "the raw slot compare"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:128
msgid ""
"Identity is the only `==` sharing can defend: with aliasing everywhere, "
"structural equality of two independently built cells is ambiguous, and "
"identity is O(1) with no deep walk. `[1, 2] == [1, 2]` is **false** — two "
"cells."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:132
msgid ""
"Enum members are immortal singletons, so `Flavor.Sour == Flavor.Sour` is "
"`true` — the one place identity quietly behaves as value."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:134
msgid ""
"`?T == ?T` is slot identity: two `nil`s are equal, a null and a box are not; "
"`p == nil` derefs the nullable side and compares against the null slot."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:137
msgid ""
"An **identity-compare lint** flags `==` between two obviously fresh "
"composites (`Vec.from([..]) == Vec.from([..])`): \"always false — compare "
"fields\". Asserting distinctness is legitimate and suppressible."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:141
msgid ""
"Field-wise comparison is a library interface contract (`hash` + `eq` spelled "
"per type) — the mechanism value-keyed maps ride. It is not connected to `==`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-dispose-identity.md:144
msgid ""
"`when` literal patterns are unaffected: arms match compile-time values, "
"never runtime `==`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:3
msgid ""
"An `interface` is a member set: signatures only, no bodies. A type satisfies "
"an interface by **having the members** — satisfaction is **structural**, "
"checked once at the positions that need it. There is nothing to register, "
"nothing to declare, and no `impl` lane for an interface: `impl I for T` does "
"not parse. Dispatch follows two positive rules, fixed at compile time per "
"call site."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:10
msgid "Interfaces"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:12
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"pub interface Shape {\n"
"    fn area(self) -> f64;         // bodiless signatures; async legal\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"pub interface Decoder<E> {\n"
"    fn decode(mut r: JsonReader) -> (Self, ?E);   // Self + generics legal\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:22
msgid ""
"**Signatures only, no bodies.** No fields, no properties, and **no default "
"implementations, ever** — one member kind, one dispatch candidate per call. "
"Anything that reads like a property is a method."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:25
msgid ""
"**`Self` and generics are legal.** `Self` in a member's signatures accepts "
"the satisfying concrete type (`-> Self` returns, `(?Self, ?E)` decodes into "
"it); a generic interface gives each type-argument list its own member set "
"(`Decoder<E>` ≠ `Decoder<F>`). Interface generic parameters take **no "
"bounds** — an interface is a flat requirement, not a constraint stack."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:31
msgid "Members carry **no `pub`** — they are as visible as the interface."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:32
msgid ""
"**No `requires` list on the interface.** Bound-taking code spells `T "
"requires Shape` at the use site; the interface itself stays a bare member "
"set."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:35
msgid ""
"**No embedding.** Interfaces are flat member sets — an interface cannot "
"embed another. Compose by declaring the members: an interface that needs two "
"capabilities spells both member sets."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:38
msgid ""
"**No object-type keyword anywhere**: an interface name in _type position_ is "
"the bare name — `s: Shape`, `Vec<Shape>`, generic arguments included."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:41
msgid ""
"**No top interface.** The erased-storage type is the concrete primitive "
"`opaque` (see [opaque — erasure and downcast](opaque.md)), reached by an "
"explicit call, never by widening."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:44
msgid ""
"**Intersection types (`A & B`) are never supported** — not deferred: "
"heterogeneous needs compose an interface that declares both member sets."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:47
msgid ""
"Interfaces may be declared in `.d.rut` declaration files — signatures-only "
"fits the declaration surface; the engine's own prelude carries none (the "
"engine's contracts are the bracket markers and the closed classes below)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:52
msgid "Satisfaction — the member-set law"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:54
msgid ""
"Satisfaction is **structural**: a type satisfies `I` exactly when its "
"**public inherent members** cover `I`'s member set. Any `pub` form counts — "
"a class method, a struct's method on its inherent impl, a newtype wrapper's "
"member. Checking, never searching: there is no registry, no registration, no "
"impl block for an interface anywhere in the language."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:61
msgid ""
"**The check runs at the boundary** — exactly the positions where a value of "
"some type `T` is used _as_ the interface:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:63
msgid ""
"**interface-typed parameters** — `dump(p)` against `fn dump(x: "
"Serializable)` checks `Point`'s member set once;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:65
msgid ""
"**typed lets and assignments** — `let s: Shape = Point { .. };` is a "
"satisfaction assertion;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:67
msgid ""
"**returns** — a function whose return type is the interface checks each "
"returned concrete;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:69
msgid ""
"**boxing sites** — pushing into a `Vec<Shape>` checks before the box is "
"built. Everywhere else the compiler never asks the question."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:72
msgid ""
"**Members must be the type's own.** The member set is the public _inherent_ "
"surface — the type's module's impl block. A third party's inherent block is "
"refused; a used type's members live where the type was declared."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:76
msgid ""
"**Signatures must match**: receiver form (`self` vs `mut self` vs none), "
"parameter types, return type, and `async` spelling — exactly, wherever both "
"sides are locally visible. `Self` in the interface's signatures accepts the "
"satisfying concrete type."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:80
msgid ""
"**Failure names all three**: passing a `Point` without `area` to a `Shape` "
"parameter diagnoses \"`Point` does not satisfy `Shape`: no member `area`\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:83
msgid ""
"**Who can never satisfy**: primitives, the composites (`[T]`, tuples), `?T`, "
"and foreign closed classes have no inherent members, so no member set — they "
"satisfy nothing. The route in is the **wrapper** (below)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:87
msgid ""
"**The empty member set is vacuously true**: an interface with no members is "
"satisfied by every type — there is no marker idiom. If a declaration needs "
"to mean something, it spells a member."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:90
msgid ""
"**Shape-equal is satisfied.** Two types whose public members have the same "
"shapes both satisfy — including types whose author never thought of the "
"interface. Accidental satisfaction is real and accepted: the mitigation is "
"convention (distinct member shapes, spelled receivers), not machinery. No "
"registration exists that could gate it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:97
msgid "`impl T { .. }` — the one impl form"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:99
msgid ""
"Type bodies are fields only; methods live in impl blocks. The inherent impl "
"is the only impl form:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:102
msgid "`impl T { .. }` (inherent)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:104
msgid "Lives in"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:104
msgid "`.rut`, **T's module only**"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:105
msgid "Valid targets"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:105
msgid "local `struct`/`class`, or a `builtin class` this module declares"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:106
msgid "`pub(..)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:106
msgid "classes only (structs are all-public)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:107
msgid "legal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:108
msgid "no-`self` methods"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:108
msgid "legal (constructors)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:109
msgid "fields / empty body"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:109
msgid "never / legal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:111
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"pub interface Serializable {\n"
"    fn encode(mut self, w: JsonWriter) -> nil;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class Point {\n"
"    x: i32;\n"
"    y: i32;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Point {\n"
"    pub fn encode(mut self, w: JsonWriter) -> nil {\n"
"        w.key(\"x\"); w.write_i64(self.x);\n"
"        w.key(\"y\"); w.write_i64(self.y);\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:129
msgid ""
"The orphan rule and the one-impl-per-pair link law are **gone** — there is "
"nothing to register, so there is nothing to duplicate and no placement rule "
"beyond the inherent one (the type's own module)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:132
msgid ""
"**Generic binders are declared after `impl`** — `impl<T> Vec<T> { .. }`, not "
"`impl Vec<T> { .. }`. The declared list is the definition site: a bare "
"parameter name in the head is a use that must resolve against it "
"(\"undeclared type parameter `T` — declare it: `impl<T> ..`\")."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:137
msgid ""
"**`Self` in impl signatures** names the impl's target under the impl's "
"substitution: `-> Self` returns, `Self { .. }` constructs."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:139
msgid ""
"An inherent impl may not carry an interface's name anywhere — there is no "
"`for` clause to spell."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:142
msgid "The wrapper route — manufacturing a member set"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:144
msgid ""
"Primitives and composites can never satisfy (no inherent members, no way to "
"add one — `impl i32 { .. }` diagnoses). The route in is a **newtype class**: "
"a one-field wrapper whose inherent impl carries the members, manufactured "
"**explicitly at every call site**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:149
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"pub interface Encodable {\n"
"    fn encode(self) -> str;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"class JsonI64(i64);\n"
"\n"
"impl JsonI64 {\n"
"    pub fn encode(self) -> str { return str(self.inner); }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn dump(x: Encodable) -> str { return x.encode(); }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let s: str = dump(JsonI64(64));   // spelled manufacture\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:167
msgid ""
"`dump(JsonI64(64))` — ✓ spelled, sound, per-call. The standard library's "
"json package spells exactly this shape for real (`encodeJson(JsonI64(64))` — "
"see [core and the swappable packages](stdlib.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:171
msgid ""
"`dump(64)` — ✗ **forever**. No auto-insertion exists at any layer: the "
"constructor call IS the manufacture, and its spelling is the audit trail."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:174
msgid ""
"Wrappers compose: a generic wrapper's bound checks at instantiation — `class "
"SWrap<T requires Encodable> { v: Vec<T>; }` admits only instantiations whose "
"argument satisfies, and the wrapper's own members let `SWrap` satisfy in "
"turn (recursive wrappers are legal)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:178
msgid ""
"The standard library's wrapper families (`JsonI64`/`JsonF64`/…, "
"`ArrFlow`/`VecFlow`/…) are exactly this shape — see [core and the swappable "
"packages](stdlib.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:182
msgid "Using an interface — the four forms"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:184
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"pub interface Shape { fn area(self) -> f64; }\n"
"\n"
"class Circle { r: f64; }\n"
"class Square { side: f64; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Circle { pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return 3.14159 * self.r * self.r; } "
"}\n"
"impl Square { pub fn area(self) -> f64 { return self.side * self.side; } }\n"
"\n"
"fn dump(x: Shape) -> f64 {            // 1. interface-typed parameter\n"
"    return x.area();\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"shapes\");\n"
"\n"
"    dump(Circle { r: 1.0 });          // 2. the boundary checks the member "
"set\n"
"\n"
"    let s: Shape = Square { side: 2.0 };   // 3. satisfaction assertion\n"
"\n"
"    let mut shapes: Vec<Shape> = Vec.new();  // 4. heterogeneous: itable "
"boxes\n"
"    shapes.push(Circle { r: 1.0 });\n"
"    shapes.push(Square { side: 3.0 });\n"
"    let mut total: f64 = 0.0;\n"
"    for (let sh of shapes) { total += sh.area(); }\n"
"    log.info(f\"total={total}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:220
msgid ""
"An interface-typed value is a reference to a real cell that still carries "
"its exact class. Passing a concrete value at an interface-typed position is "
"implicit and allocates nothing; the compiler checks the member set at that "
"boundary and moves on — there is no widening relation to maintain and no "
"registry row to consult. An interface-typed value **cannot be downcast**: "
"use it through the interface, or erase explicitly through `opaque`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:228
msgid "Dispatch — the two-rule law"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:230
msgid ""
"Every method call compiles under exactly one of two rules; the rule is a "
"property of the call site, fixed at compile time."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:233
msgid ""
"**Static** — the call site names exactly one concrete type; the call binds "
"directly to the member, no table hop:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:235
msgid "a concrete receiver — `c.area()` on `c: Circle`;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:236
msgid ""
"an interface-typed local of **single concrete origin** — `let s: Shape = "
"Point { .. }; s.area()` binds straight to `Point`'s member;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:239
msgid ""
"an interface-typed **parameter** — the callee specializes per concrete "
"argument type (one clone per argument type — finite, terminating), so an "
"interface parameter _is_ an implicit generic bound;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:243
msgid ""
"a monomorphized generic — inside `fn first<T>(..)`, `T`'s members are static "
"per instantiation."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:248
msgid ""
"heterogeneous container elements — `for (s of shapes)` over a `Vec<Shape>`;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:250
msgid "interface-typed field/element loads;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:251
msgid ""
"branch-merged origins — `let s = if (c) { a } else { b };` where the arms "
"carry different concretes into one interface-typed binding."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:255
msgid ""
"The itable rows are synthesized **at the boxing sites where satisfaction was "
"proved** — the same boundary check that admitted the value fills the row. "
"Origin counting is conservative: any merge, indirection load, or "
"cross-function flow counts as multiple. Mis-analysis cannot produce wrong "
"code — an uncertain origin costs an itable hop, never a wrong static bind."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:262
msgid ""
"**The use-both gate**: `x.iface_member()` requires **both** names at the "
"call site's module — the type (by declaration or `use`) _and_ the interface "
"(`use`). A call through an interface no `use` names is an error — \"use `I` "
"to call its methods on `T`\" — so a foreign interface can never silently "
"change what a call site means."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:268
msgid "Type tests — `is`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:270
msgid ""
"`expr is Type` → `bool`, at relational precedence, non-associative. The "
"right-hand side is a naming position: a concrete type or a bare interface "
"name/instantiation."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:274
msgid ""
"**Concrete RHS — exact-type test**: true when the value's exact class or "
"struct _is_ `T`. With no inheritance this is a single descriptor lookup."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:277
msgid ""
"**Interface RHS — capability probe**: true when the value's concrete type "
"has itable fills for that interface — which is exactly when the value was "
"satisfaction-boxed somewhere (`k is Serializable`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:280
msgid ""
"**No flow sensitivity**: `if (x is Shape) { .. }` grants nothing — no "
"narrowing, no widening. The keyword answers; it does not admit."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:282
msgid ""
"**Static folds**: when the receiver's static type already answers, the "
"result is a compile-time constant, with an always-true/false lint. On an "
"exact receiver the member set decides at compile time."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:285
msgid ""
"`is` is total: never traps, yields only `bool`. The same descriptor that "
"answers the itable answers the probe — one runtime truth per value."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:289
msgid "The iteration protocol — the `[iterable]` marker"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:291
msgid "A type is iterable when an inherent impl marks a member `[iterable]`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:293
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"class CountUp {\n"
"    n: i32;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl CountUp {\n"
"    pub fn new(n: i32) -> Self { return Self { n: n }; }\n"
"\n"
"    [iterable] pub fn iterate(self, emit: fn(i32) -> bool) {\n"
"        for (let i = 1; i <= self.n; i += 1) {\n"
"            if (!emit(i)) { return; }\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    for (let v of CountUp.new(3)) {\n"
"        log.info(f\"tick {v}\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:324
msgid ""
"The contract's shape: `fn <free>(self, emit: fn(E) -> bool)` — the member's "
"NAME IS FREE (the bracket designates, never the spelling), and the element "
"type `E` falls out of the marked member's own emit parameter. `for (v of it) "
"{ body }` calls that ONE designated member — `it.<member>(emit)` — with a "
"synthetic closure: the body runs, then `emit` returns `true`; `break` "
"returns `false` (stopping the iteration); `continue` returns `true` "
"immediately; a `return` inside the body stops the iteration (not the "
"enclosing function). The loop variable is the closure's parameter — a fresh "
"binding per iteration; captured enclosing locals are copied by value at the "
"desugar, so accumulate through a shared cell or a method. The builtin "
"sequences (`[T]`, `Vec<T>`, `str`, `bytes`) keep their fused index loops and "
"never reach the marker. An enum value iterates the same way — a marked "
"member on the enum's impl makes `for (let v of c)` walk whatever it emits "
"(see [Enums](enums.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:340
msgid "Engine contracts — markers and closed classes"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:342
msgid ""
"The engine's own contracts stand alone — they are neither interfaces nor "
"user-visible polymorphism, and they share no machinery with the member-set "
"law. Two mechanisms, one job each:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:346
msgid ""
"**The bracket markers** — `[disposal]` and `[iterable]` designate an "
"inherent impl member. The parser accepts any contextual word in the "
"brackets; the checker validates the engine's CLOSED set, at most one member "
"per contract per class, inherent-members-only, and each contract's "
"signature. The descriptor ABI is a designated slot per class — the engine's "
"release path and the for-of weave read their slot directly, never an itable "
"lookup:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:354
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"impl Db {\n"
"    [disposal] fn dispose_db(mut self, cx: DisposalContext) {  // name FREE "
"—\n"
"        self.file.close();                                      // the "
"bracket\n"
"    }                                                           // "
"designates\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Rows {\n"
"    [iterable] fn iterate(self, emit: fn(Row) -> bool) { .. }   // for-of "
"reads\n"
"}                                                               // this "
"slot\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:366
msgid ""
"`[disposal]`'s contract: `fn <free>(mut self, cx: DisposalContext)` — the "
"engine calls it when a value of the type reaches refcount zero ([the Rc "
"heap](rc-heap.md)); the target must be a CONCRETE struct or class (the row "
"keys the cell's type id). `[iterable]`'s contract is the iteration protocol "
"above."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:372
msgid ""
"**The closed builtin classes** — `Future<T>` and `RunContext` carry the "
"async protocol. Both are `pub builtin` (the import-gated spelling) and "
"**closed**: no constructor, no impl lane, no user-callable members beyond "
"`RunContext`'s two reads — a user type cannot BE a future or a cx. The walls "
"hold by nominal closure: the member-set law cannot see engine identity, so "
"closure is the only spelling of \"engine-minted only\". The ENGINE weaves "
"without them — async frames, the minted cx, and the fused `for..of` loops "
"never consult user scope; source that SPELLS a name resolves it only through "
"`use core::{ .. }` (a launcher's `f: Future<T>`, `downcast<Future<..>>`, a "
"`cx` probe in an async body — see [Async and await](async.md), [host fns and "
"declaration files](host-fns.md), and [the Rc heap](rc-heap.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:386
msgid "Accepted debts"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:388
msgid "The model trades two guarantees for its one mechanism, on purpose:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:390
msgid ""
"**Shape-equal satisfaction.** Two types with public members of the same "
"shapes both satisfy an interface, whether or not either author intended it. "
"There is no opt-in to fence this — the mitigation is convention (distinct "
"member shapes, spelled receivers), documented here as accepted debt."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:395
msgid ""
"**Wrappers at every primitive boundary.** Because primitives never satisfy, "
"every polymorphic hand-off of a primitive spells a wrapper construction — "
"`dump(JsonI64(64))`, per call site. The spelling is the price of having no "
"auto-insertion; the payback is that a wrapper's presence is greppable and "
"its cost is visible."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:400
msgid ""
"**Bare containers never derive.** A `[T]`, a tuple, or a `?T` will never "
"grow members; container polymorphism always goes through an explicit wrapper "
"family (`JsonArr<T>`, `ArrFlow<T>`, …), never a compiler-derived instance."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:405
msgid "Equality"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/interfaces.md:407
msgid ""
"`==` is a builtin operator with no dynamic dispatch: primitives by value, "
"`str`/`bytes` by content, everything else by cell identity — see [Rc, "
"dispose, and identity](rc-dispose-identity.md). Field-wise comparison is an "
"interface-shaped contract implemented per type; it is not connected to `==`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:3
msgid ""
"Function declarations, anonymous closures, and monomorphized generics with "
"admission-only bounds."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:6 src/reference/stdlib.md:33
msgid "Functions"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:8
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\n"
"    return a + b;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"{add(2, 3)}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:25
msgid ""
"Parameters are `name: Type`; a mutable parameter declares `mut name` — the "
"callee's permission to mutate the caller's object (see [Modules and "
"visibility](modules-and-visibility.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:28
msgid ""
"A function without a result arrow returns `nil`; `return;` (or falling off "
"the end) is its return."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:30
msgid ""
"Methods are functions in impl blocks: the first parameter spelled `self` (or "
"`mut self`) makes it an instance method; absence of `self` makes it a class "
"method (see [Classes and constructors](classes.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:34
msgid ""
"`entry fn` publishes a function to the embedder (see [Modules and "
"visibility](modules-and-visibility.md)); `async fn` declares a suspending "
"function (see [Async and await](async.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:37
msgid ""
"Function types are first-class: `fn apply(f: fn(i32) -> i32, v: i32) -> i32`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:39
msgid "Closures"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:41
msgid ""
"The closure spelling is an **anonymous fn** — block bodies, no arrow form:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:44
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let add = fn (a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { return a + b; };\n"
"    let area_of = fn (r: f32) -> f32 {\n"
"        let sq = r * r;\n"
"        return sq * 3.14159265f32;\n"
"    };\n"
"    log.info(f\"add={add(1, 2)} area={area_of(1)}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:62
msgid ""
"An anonymous fn inhabits `fn(P..) -> R` directly — it is a value of the "
"function type, copyable like a primitive."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:64
msgid ""
"**The capture law**: primitives, `nil`, and `fn` values copy — a captured "
"scalar is the closure's own slot, and reassigning it inside the closure "
"never moves the original. Ref-headed values (records, `Vec`, arrays, "
"`str`/`bytes`, `?T`, enums) cross as handles: writes through the captured "
"handle reach the shared cell, and so does a whole-value REASSIGNMENT — a "
"captured-and-reassigned binding is promoted to a hidden shared-slot cell, so "
"both frames stay linked for the binding's whole scope (a "
"stash-then-reassign, a reassign inside the closure, and the `for..of` loop "
"variable all follow this one law). Refcounting keeps captures alive; a "
"closure is itself a shared cell value."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:75
msgid ""
"Closures are not transferable across isolates (a worker boundary transfers "
"values, not closures)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:80
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"fn first<T>(xs: [T], fallback: T) -> T {\n"
"    if (xs.len() == 0) { return fallback; }\n"
"    return xs[0];\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let head = first([10, 20], -1);      // first<i32>   — monomorphized\n"
"    let name = first([\"a\", \"b\"], \"?\");   // first<str>   — separate "
"instance\n"
"    log.info(f\"{head} {name}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:100
msgid ""
"**Generics monomorphize at compile time**: each instantiation emits its own "
"typed code. `T` infers from the arguments; explicit type arguments may be "
"spelled at call sites, including method calls: `self.st.get<T>(self)`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:104
msgid ""
"Interface-typed arguments are ordinary arguments: a `T` instantiated at an "
"interface type becomes a handle slot, satisfying no bound."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:106
msgid ""
"**Generic parameters are unconstrained by default** — you cannot call "
"methods on a bare `T`. Pass values in, take an `I`\\-typed parameter, or "
"spell the bound (`T requires I`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:109
msgid ""
"There are no const-generic user parameters. The builtin surfaces fix their "
"shapes (`[T]` is one type; lengths are runtime values)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:112
msgid "Inline bounds — `requires`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:119
msgid ""
"`fn f<T requires A | B>(x: T)` — an **admission-only** bound on fn, method, "
"and class generic parameters (struct and interface generic parameters reject "
"`requires`):"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:123
msgid ""
"The bound gates which instantiations compile: enforcement is at every "
"substitution-completing site (free-fn calls, method instantiation, "
"impl-method enqueue). A failing instantiation diagnoses with the bound "
"spelled out — \"`bool` does not satisfy `T` requires `i32 | str`\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:128
msgid ""
"Members may be concrete type names (satisfied by exact type identity), "
"aliases (expanded first — see [Type aliases and union "
"bounds](type-aliases.md)), a single interface (satisfied structurally — the "
"member set checks where `T` is chosen), or a **type union** of concrete "
"names/aliases. An interface member inside a union spelling is invalid — an "
"interface bound stands alone."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:135
msgid ""
"**Interface-typed values satisfy nothing**: a `T` instantiated at an "
"interface type fails any bound — only a concrete type with the members "
"admits."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:138
msgid ""
"A non-union bound **proves the widening**: the body may widen a `T`\\-typed "
"value into a bound-interface-typed slot (`let w: Labeled = x;`), but gains "
"no method calls on bare `T`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:141
msgid ""
"A **union bound** carries the whole-bound contract: a method call on a "
"union-bounded value requires **every** member to provide the method — even a "
"member that is never actually instantiated. Dispatch is untouched: each "
"instantiation still binds the concrete member's own method."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:146
msgid ""
"Bounds may reference the item's other generics: `fn hold<T, U requires "
"[T]>(x: U)`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/functions-closures-generics.md:148
msgid ""
"Generic **classes** take bounds on their parameters — the bound records on "
"the class descriptor and admits every instantiation: `pub class HashMap<K "
"requires i8 | .. | bytes, V>` (see [Builtin generic "
"types](builtin-generic-types.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:3
msgid ""
"`opaque` is the erasure-box engine primitive: a concrete type whose values "
"box any value — **forgetting the static type and keeping the runtime type "
"for downcast only**. Erasure is a call you write; the static type system "
"never loosens, and rut has no cast syntax for it (`as` is the numeric cast "
"only)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:11
msgid ""
"`opaque` is a declared engine primitive, **ambient** — no `use` gates it. "
"Its entire API:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:16
msgid "`opaque(v)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:16
msgid "erasure: box any value; answers the `opaque` box"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:17
msgid "`opaque.downcast<T>(o) -> ?T`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:17
msgid "checked recovery: the box's inner cell on a match, `nil` on a mismatch"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:18
msgid "`x is opaque`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:18
msgid "the ordinary concrete test for the box type itself"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:20
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }\n"
"enum Flavor { Sour, Sweet }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let box1 = opaque(Point { x: 1, y: 2 });   // erasure = call of the type "
"name\n"
"    let p = opaque.downcast<Point>(box1);      // ?Point — the nullable\n"
"    when (p != nil) {\n"
"        true -> { log.info(f\"point {p.x} {p.y}\"); },\n"
"        else  -> { log.info(\"point: nil\"); },\n"
"    }\n"
"    let wrong = opaque.downcast<i32>(opaque(Flavor.Sour));  // nil, no trap\n"
"    log.info(f\"wrong == nil: {wrong == nil}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:46
msgid ""
"**Construction is erasure.** Every value can become `opaque`. Under the "
"all-cells regime, boxing is **zero-copy**: the box stores the payload's cell "
"handle, so mutations through the original name are visible through the box "
"(the alias law, see [By-reference and "
"nullable](by-reference-and-nullable.md)). A primitive payload copies the "
"bits — value semantics where aliasing is unobservable:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:54
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let mut n = 5;\n"
"    let b = opaque(n);    // a prim payload COPIES the bits\n"
"    n = 9;                // ...so the source moving stays out\n"
"    log.info(f\"{opaque.downcast<i32>(b)} vs {n}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:70
msgid ""
"**A match is the box's own inner cell.** The recovered `?T` shares the box: "
"writes through the recovery are the source's, in both directions, through "
"every holder. A primitive payload reads the copied value."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:73
msgid ""
"**A mismatch is `nil`.** There is no flag and no zero value — the miss is "
"the nullable's null; an unguarded dereference traps `NilDeref`, never a "
"silent zero."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:76
msgid ""
"**The type argument must be concrete.** An interface-typed type argument "
"(`opaque.downcast<Drawable>`) is a compile error — interface-typed values "
"have no recovery path by design."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:79
msgid ""
"**The opaque-is law: `is` names the box, never the payload.** `o is T` and "
"`o is I` answer **by the box** — false for every payload type, concrete and "
"interface alike. The one check that names what it is — `o is opaque` — is "
"`true`. Recovery is `downcast<T>` only. On a non-box receiver, `x is opaque` "
"is the ordinary concrete test for the box type."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:86
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let b = opaque(\"hello\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"{b is str} {opaque.downcast<str>(b) != nil}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:100
msgid ""
"**Boxes are never equal unless identical**: `opaque(v) == opaque(v)` is "
"`false` — two boxes, two cells. Box once, compare boxes."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:102
msgid ""
"**Interface-typed values cannot be boxed**: erasure takes a concrete value. "
"Fixed arrays box fine; their identity covers the element type."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:105
msgid "Composition and costs"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:107
msgid ""
"`opaque` is a concrete primitive, not a lattice node: it composes in every "
"type position trivially — `Vec<opaque>`, `[opaque]`, fields, returns, "
"parameters."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:111
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"use pouch::{ Vec };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let boxes: [opaque] = [opaque(Point { x: 3, y: 4 }), opaque(\"two\")];\n"
"    let vec: Vec<opaque> = Vec.from([opaque(5)]);\n"
"    log.info(f\"{boxes.len()} {vec.len()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:129
msgid ""
"The rut-side box is one small cell: the erased value rides inline in the "
"box, and cell churn is recycled by the heap arena. Heap accounting charges "
"the box's cell, as always (see [Reified types and layout](reified-types.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:133
msgid ""
"The host side of the same surface: an embedder can mint the box over host "
"data with no static rut shape. rut sees only the box — `o is opaque` is "
"`true`, `o is T` misses for every rut `T`, and `opaque.downcast<T>` answers "
"`nil` for every `T` (the miss is checked, never a trap). The host borrows "
"the payload back typed, call-scoped and borrow-guarded."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:139
msgid ""
"Crossing isolates is allowed iff the boxed value's type is crossable, "
"checked at runtime via the type descriptor."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/opaque.md:141
msgid ""
"An `opaque` box can do nothing until it is recovered — no methods, no "
"fields, no format-string rendering. That is the difference from gradual "
"typing: the erased-storage type for a hot loop is a smell; lints flag "
"downcasts inside loop bodies and `opaque` parameters/returns on non-storage "
"functions."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:3
msgid ""
"Every value's type is available at runtime as a type descriptor. Slots are "
"untagged 8-byte values; bytecode is typed, so hot paths carry no tags. This "
"page is the language-facing contract; the descriptor itself is VM data, not "
"a script value."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:8
msgid "Where reification is language-facing"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:10 src/reference/string-views.md:8
msgid "Surface"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:10
msgid "Mechanism"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:12
msgid "`is` type tests — concrete and interface RHS"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:12
msgid ""
"the value cell's descriptor (see [Interfaces and dispatch](interfaces.md))"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:13
msgid "`opaque.downcast<T>` recovery"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:13
msgid ""
"the box's recorded runtime type (see [opaque — erasure and "
"downcast](opaque.md))"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:14 src/reference/reified-types.md:18
msgid "`type_id<T>()`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:14
msgid "the compile-time type-identity constant"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:15
msgid "host boundary checks"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:15
msgid "every crossing is checked against the declared parameter type"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:16
msgid "diagnostics"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:16
msgid "stack traces and host tooling read the same descriptors"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:20
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let TID_POINT: u32 = type_id<Point>();\n"
"    log.info(f\"{TID_POINT} eq={type_id<Point>() == TID_POINT}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:36
msgid ""
"`type_id<T>() -> u32` — the identity of the **instantiated** type: unique "
"per VM run, stable across modules, comparable only. `Vec<f32>` ≠ `Vec<f64>`; "
"`Point` = `Point` wherever declared."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:39
msgid ""
"It is a **compile-time constant** — a load-time expression (legal in "
"module-level `let` initializers, see [Modules and "
"visibility](modules-and-visibility.md)), folded from the type table, never "
"executed."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:43
msgid ""
"Value size and alignment are implementation details, not a language surface."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:45
msgid ""
"Constructing a value _from_ raw bytes is deliberately not provided: it could "
"forge private fields and class invariants."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:48
msgid "Value representation — slot arrays"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:50
msgid ""
"Every struct and class value lives in a heap **cell**: header + (vtable, "
"when the type has impls) + the payload."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:53
msgid ""
"The payload is a **slot array: one untagged 8-byte slot per field, in "
"declaration order**. Primitive fields are widened into their slot "
"(sign/zero-extended; a float is stored at the canonical 64-bit width); "
"composite fields are cell-handle slots."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:57
msgid ""
"Visibility, generic parameters, and impl blocks add **nothing** — the "
"payload depends only on the field list. Field access is by index; heap "
"accounting charges `fields × 8` bytes."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:60
msgid ""
"Buffers of primitive elements (`Vec<f32>`, `[i32]`) stay **flat**, packed to "
"the element's machine width; composite elements are one handle slot each — "
"`Vec<Point>` stores one handle per element."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:63
msgid ""
"Enums are tagged cells (a tag slot plus a payload slot); dataless enum "
"variants are immortal singleton cells."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:65
msgid ""
"Nullable `?prim` element storage inside sequence backings is the raw payload "
"plus a one-byte nil tag — no per-element cell."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:67
msgid ""
"`bytes` at the engine level is a `u8` array cell; a `str` is an immutable, "
"COW-shared UTF-8 block, and a slice of it is a small view cell (see [String "
"slicing and views](string-views.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:71
msgid "Cells and vtables"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:78
msgid ""
"The **exact runtime type** lives in the cell (via the vtable when present, "
"the header otherwise). Every cell is minted at construction with its vtable "
"already attached."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:81
msgid ""
"Interface member ids are assigned **globally per interface instantiation** "
"at compile time (`Slice<Point>` ≠ `Slice<str>`); a type's vtable fills every "
"slot of every interface instantiation it was satisfaction-boxed at — the "
"fill is synthesized by the boxing site's boundary check, and a type that "
"never crosses an interface-typed boundary fills nothing."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:87
msgid ""
"Widening a composite to an interface `I` **reuses the same cell and "
"vtable**: the interface-typed value is the handle plus the vtable pointer — "
"no allocation, no copy. The vtable reserves no base-prefix room: there is no "
"inheritance."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:91
msgid ""
"A call through an interface object is two loads and an indirect jump (the "
"receiver's vtable, the member slot). Interface members never devirtualize; "
"inherent calls bind directly:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:95
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"Op::CallI { recv, slot: 3, args }   // s.area() — itable slot 3\n"
"Op::Call     { func: \"Circle$area\", recv, args }   // c.area() — inherent\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:100
msgid "The type test"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:102
msgid ""
"`is` with a concrete right-hand side lowers to: load the object's exact type "
"id, compare — a compile-time constant comparison when the static type "
"already answers. The interface-RHS capability probe is: exact-type compare, "
"then a flat scan of the descriptor's filled itable slots — no inheritance "
"chain to walk (see [Interfaces and dispatch](interfaces.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:109
msgid ""
"`opaque` interacts with exactly one of these reads: `is` reads the **box's** "
"own type, so every payload probe misses; only `opaque.downcast<T>`'s match "
"test keeps reading the payload — the one legitimate see-through."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:114
msgid "Boxing"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/reified-types.md:116
msgid ""
"Storing a value into an erasure box widens by the slot discipline: an "
"integer box stores the 64-bit sign/zero-extended value, a float the 64-bit "
"width. A kind check plus `opaque.downcast<i64>()` / `downcast<f64>()` / "
"`downcast<bool>()` / `downcast<str>()` is therefore total in-branch: within "
"the successful branch the value's type is the exact concrete type, and "
"everything downstream optimizes as if the value had never been erased."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:3
msgid ""
"`s.slice(from, to)` — the O(1) string window. A slice is a **view**: no "
"octets move or copy; the new `str` cell retains the original and records a "
"byte window. Slicing is safe because `str` is immutable — a view can never "
"observe mutation, so no pointer spelling is needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:10
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let s = \"hello world\";\n"
"    let w = s.slice(6, 11);        // \"world\" — no copy\n"
"    log.info(w);\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:25
msgid ""
"`from`/`to` are **codepoint indices**, `from <= to <= s.len()`; bounds "
"violations trap (same as an out-of-range index)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:27
msgid ""
"The window is recorded internally in **byte offsets** — codepoint bounds are "
"resolved once at slice time (a UTF-8 walk only when the string is non-ASCII)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:30
msgid ""
"The result is an ordinary `str`: it prints, compares by content (`==`), "
"iterates (`for (c of w)`), renders in f-strings, and `len()` counts its own "
"codepoints. **There is no separate view type on the surface** — a slice of a "
"`str` _is_ a `str`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:34
msgid ""
"Binding the slice shares it like every cell (see [By-reference and "
"nullable](by-reference-and-nullable.md)); `str` has no clone — a "
"materializing copy happens only where an operation needs one (the append "
"fast path, the host crossing)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:39
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let accented = \"héllo!\";\n"
"    log.info(f\"{accented.len()} {accented.encode().len()} "
"{accented.slice(1, 2)}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:53
msgid "The view cell"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:55
msgid ""
"A slice mints a small view cell: `{ parent, off, len, ascii }` — a "
"**retained** handle to the owned parent string plus byte offsets into its "
"block."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:59
msgid ""
"The view charges only its own header (~32 bytes) against the heap budget; "
"the parent's octets stay alive as long as any view does (deterministic "
"destruction: view rc-0 → parent release)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:62
msgid ""
"**View-of-view flattens**: slicing a view combines offsets onto the root, so "
"the parent is always an owned string and reads never chain."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:64
msgid ""
"The ascii flag is inherited from the root at slice time (a window of an "
"ASCII string is ASCII)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:67
msgid "Reads go through, writes never do"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:69
msgid ""
"Every string operation reads through views transparently: content equality, "
"codepoint count, indexing, iteration, f-string rendering, `encode`, and the "
"host boundary (which copies the window's bytes out)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:73
msgid ""
"The in-place append fast path (the `out = f\"{out}..\"` accumulator) is "
"gated to **owned, uniquely-referenced** cells. Concatenating a view copies "
"its bytes out — `f\"{view}!\"` yields a fresh owned string, and the view "
"(and its parent) are unchanged. No operation can ever mutate through a "
"string view. The accumulator is linear in the total output — core ships no "
"string-builder class; a long-lived builder is the `strbuild` package's "
"`StringBuilder` ([stdlib](stdlib.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:81
msgid "Cost model"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:83
msgid "Operation"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:83
msgid "Cost"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:85
msgid "`s.slice(a, b)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:85
msgid "O(1) — one small cell + one retain (UTF-8 walk only for non-ASCII bounds)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:86
msgid "read/compare/render a view"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:86
msgid "O(window) — same as any str"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:87
msgid "concat out / the host crossing"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:87
msgid "O(window) — the materializing copy"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:88
msgid "parsing a 1 KiB line out of a 1 MiB buffer"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:88
msgid "one 32-byte cell, zero copies"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:90
msgid ""
"Digest-style workloads (splitting, tokenizing, windowing) stop copying "
"entirely; peak heap reports the buffer once instead of per-piece."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:93
msgid "`[T]` windows"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:95
msgid ""
"`v.slice(from, to)` on a `Vec<T>` (and on `[T]`) mints a fixed-length window "
"over the backing array, boxed as **`?Vec<T>`** (the `T → ?T` coercion — see "
"[Builtin generic types](builtin-generic-types.md)). The window **is** the "
"shared cell:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:100
msgid ""
"reads: `w[i]`, `w.len()`, `for (x of w)` — auto-deref the nullable and go "
"through the window (element `i` is `parent[off + i]`, bounds are the "
"window's);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:103
msgid "writes: `w[i] = x` (and compound assignment) **hit the parent**;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:104
msgid ""
"**fixed-length**: `push`/`pop` through a view trap — copy the elements out "
"to grow (a `for`\\-push loop does it);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:106
msgid "reslicing flattens onto the root backing (`w.slice(a, b)`);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:107
msgid ""
"parent growth **detaches**: `push` re-backs the `Vec` with a fresh array; "
"the window keeps pinning the old backing — the same aliasing rule Go slices "
"have;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:110
msgid ""
"iteration yields the stored elements as-is under the sharing law: a cell "
"element shares its cell, so a write through the loop variable hits the "
"parent; primitive elements copy out as slots always do."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/string-views.md:114
msgid ""
"The asymmetry is deliberate: `str`/`bytes` are immutable, so their views "
"carry no mutability law — purely an optimization plus a nicer parsing "
"surface. Array windows change what writes mean, which is why they surface as "
"an explicit `?Vec<T>` value while the string view is invisible."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:3
msgid ""
"`type X = A;` — the transparent alias. `type X = A | B;` — the bound-only "
"union. `fn f<T requires A | B>(..)` — the inline admission-only bound. All "
"three are compile-time gates: no IR, no runtime representation."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:8
msgid "Type aliases"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:14
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"type Meters = i64;\n"
"type Km = Meters;             // chains expand: Km is Meters is i64\n"
"type Row = [i32];\n"
"\n"
"let trip: Km = 1500;          // Km is Meters is i64 — all one type\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let plain: Meters = trip;     // no conversion: the alias IS the target\n"
"    log.info(f\"{trip} {plain}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:34
msgid ""
"**Transparency by construction**: `X` resolves to the target's type identity "
"everywhere — params, fields, returns, lets, bounds. A value of the alias and "
"a value of the target are the same type; there is no conversion and no "
"wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:38
msgid ""
"Chains expand through resolution; `type A = B; type B = A;` is a \"recursive "
"type alias\" error. Forward references are legal."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:40
msgid ""
"The alias **target validates at declaration**: `type Foo = NotAType;` is an "
"error whether or not `Foo` is used."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:42
msgid ""
"**Non-generic**: the head admits no members. `type Vec2<T> = Vec<T>;` is not "
"expressible — a generic alias would be a type constructor, not a transparent "
"name."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:45
msgid ""
"**One name = one type**: an alias may not share its name with an alias, "
"enum, struct, class, or interface in the same module — the duplicate-name "
"check is symmetric and fires in both orders."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:48
msgid ""
"Visibility: `pub`, `pub(mod)`, `pub(super)`, `pub(self)` all parse on `type` "
"(see [Modules and visibility](modules-and-visibility.md)). Like every name, "
"the alias is usable in a consumer only when the consumer's `use` names it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:53
msgid "Union aliases — bound-only"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:55
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"unionalias := typealias   // with Type spelled `A | B`\n"
"bound      := Type ('|' Type)*\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:60
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"type Num = i32 | str;    // legal — here and in requires bounds ONLY\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:64
msgid ""
"The union exists only in the type **grammar**; the compiler never interns a "
"union runtime kind. There is no runtime union value, no subtyping, and no "
"`is`/`when` support over unions (heterogeneous data goes through interfaces "
"— see [Interfaces and dispatch](interfaces.md) — or enums, see "
"[Enums](enums.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:70
msgid ""
"**Value positions reject unions**: a union (or a union-alias name) in a "
"param/field/return/let/`is` position is diagnosed as _bound-only_."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:72
msgid ""
"`|` continues a completed type as a union only where unions are legal — the "
"alias target and `requires` bounds — so `x as u32 | y` keeps spelling the "
"binary operator."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:75
msgid ""
"Union aliases are **module-local**: never exported; a cross-module use fails "
"as an unknown type."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:78
msgid "Inline `requires` — the admission-only bound"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:84
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"interface Labeled { fn label(self) -> str; }\n"
"struct Ridge { depth: i32; }\n"
"struct Trench { depth: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Ridge { fn label(self) -> str { return \"ridge\"; } }\n"
"impl Trench { fn label(self) -> str { return \"trench\"; } }\n"
"\n"
"fn name<T requires Labeled>(x: T) -> str {\n"
"    let w: Labeled = x;      // the widening compiles BECAUSE the bound "
"holds\n"
"    return w.label();\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn kind<T requires Ridge | Trench>(x: T) -> str { return \"geo\"; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"{name(Ridge { depth: 3 })} {kind(Trench { depth: 1 })}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:111
msgid ""
"Members may be concrete type names (satisfied by exact type identity at "
"instantiation), aliases (expanded before detection), or a **type union** of "
"those. **Type names only in a union**: an interface member inside a union "
"spelling parses but diagnoses at admission — an interface bound must stand "
"alone."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:116
msgid ""
"**Enforcement at every substitution-completing site**: free-fn calls, direct "
"and instance method instantiation, and impl-method enqueue. A failing "
"instantiation diagnoses with the bound spelled out: \"`bool` does not "
"satisfy `T` requires `i32 | str` — no matching type in the bound\". An "
"interface bound fails with the member set: \"`T`'s instantiation does not "
"satisfy `Labeled`: no member `label`\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:122
msgid ""
"**Interface-typed values satisfy nothing**: instantiating `T` at an "
"interface type fails any bound — only a concrete type with the members "
"admits."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:125
msgid ""
"**Bounds may reference the item's other generics**: `fn hold<T, U requires "
"[T]>(x: U)` — members resolve under the call-site substitution."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:128
msgid ""
"**Admission-only — except the union's whole-bound contract.** A non-union "
"bound grants NO method calls on bare `T`; what it proves is the widening "
"into a bound-member-typed slot. A **union bound** carries the whole-bound "
"method contract: a method call on a union-bounded value is checked against "
"the WHOLE bound — every member must provide the method through its own impls "
"— even when the offending member is never instantiated. Dispatch is "
"untouched: each instantiation still binds the concrete member's own impl."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:136
msgid ""
"**Provenance** — a value is known to carry a union-bounded type when it is: "
"a generic parameter, a `let` whose annotation spells the generic, a copy of "
"either, or a direct `self.f` field whose declared type spells the generic. "
"Provenance rides inlining and is cleared when the name rebinds. Documented "
"holes (the gate under-fires, never over-fires): closure bodies compile with "
"fresh provenance; destructuring carries none; a shadowing rebind resets it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:143
msgid "**Class generics take bounds; struct/interface/surface generics do not**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:149
msgid ""
"The bound records on the class descriptor and admits every instantiation: a "
"key type outside the set fails **at the instantiation site** (see [Builtin "
"generic types](builtin-generic-types.md)). Inside the class's method bodies "
"the union contract applies through the class's own `requires`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/type-aliases.md:156
msgid ""
"See also [Functions, closures, and generics](functions-closures-generics.md) "
"for the bound grammar in context."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:3
msgid ""
"The sharing regime, the nullable `?T`, identity `==`, and the one copy "
"escape hatch. These laws landed together and they are not optional: every "
"other page assumes them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:7
msgid "The sharing regime"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:9
msgid ""
"**Primitives and `fn` values copy** (immediate slots): `u8..u64`, `i8..i64`, "
"`f32`/`f64`, `bool`, and closures' function values."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:11
msgid ""
"**Every cell type shares its cell**: `str`, `bytes`, struct/class records, "
"`[T]` arrays, enums, interface objects, `opaque` boxes, closures' captured "
"cells, and `?T` boxes."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:14
msgid ""
"`let b = a` — any cell type — is an O(1) handle move (retain new, release "
"old). Mutation through any alias is visible through all of them."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:18
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let mut p = Point { x: 1, y: 2 };\n"
"    let q = p;              // SHARE: one cell, two names — no copy\n"
"    p.x = 4;                // q.x is 4 now\n"
"    log.info(f\"{q.x}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:36
msgid ""
"Writing is gated by the **`mut`\\-binding law**, never by the sharing: a "
"`let` binding is a read-only view; `let mut` grants write access to the "
"object it holds (see [Modules and visibility](modules-and-visibility.md)). "
"Parameters declare the same intent: `fn step(mut p: Point)` writes the "
"caller's point; `fn area(p: Point)` promises not to."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:42
msgid ""
"A `T` stored into a `?T` slot boxes once — the box **aliases** the payload's "
"cell (a share); primitives and `nil` copy the bits. There is no "
"deref-on-load/box-on-store special case anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:45
msgid ""
"Primitive elements in a `[T]` stay flat slots; composite elements are handle "
"slots. `for (let x of xs)` yields the **shared** element, not a per-element "
"copy — a cell element writes through to the sequence."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:48
msgid ""
"The repeat `[v; n]` retains the handle `n` times: every slot aliases the one "
"cell. The repeat never copies."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:51
msgid ""
"Copy-by-value is unobservable except through the two legal effects: "
"**aliasing** (writes visible through every binding) and `==` (below)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:54
msgid "`?T` — the nullable type"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:56
msgid ""
"`?T` is a nil-able cell — a one-slot box. `nil` is the null literal; "
"dereferencing it traps `NilDeref` — never a silent read."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:59
msgid "Grammar — prefix only, binds tightest"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:66
msgid "the same runtime box, unwrapped transitively"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:68
msgid ""
"There is no postfix `T?` and no `*T` — each is a diagnosed error naming "
"`?T`. In expression position, `*x`/`&x` diagnose: \"bindings share by "
"reference now — pass `x` directly\" (binary `*`/`&` operators are untouched)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:73
msgid "Coercions and uses"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:75
msgid ""
"**`T → ?T` boxes; `?T → T` derefs** — both implicit at the expected-type "
"position, transitive through `??T`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:80
msgid ""
"`p == nil` / `p != nil` compare against the null slot; guard before use. The "
"trap is the bug-catcher, not the semantics."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:82
msgid ""
"A `?T` binding IS the cell reference: writes through it hit the shared cell "
"(a `nudge(mut pt: ?Point)` moves the caller's point)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:84
msgid ""
"`nil` typing: a context-free `nil` has type `nil`; in an expected-`?T` "
"position it types as that `?T` — so `let p: ?Node = nil` and a `left: nil` "
"field in a literal just work. Absence reads plainly: a lookup returns `?V`, "
"and `nil` means \"not found\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:89
msgid ""
"When the cell's refcount reaches zero, the engine runs the type's "
"`[disposal]` member — the cell-death hook (see [Rc, dispose, and "
"identity](rc-dispose-identity.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:92
msgid ""
"Across the host boundary `?T` crosses nil-flattened when its element crosses "
"— an `entry fn -> (?T, err)` is a first-class host answer."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:95
msgid "`==` — identity for cells, content for text"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:99
msgid "IEEE 754 for floats (`NaN != NaN`, `-0.0 == 0.0`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:102
msgid ""
"everything else — records, arrays, enums, closures, interface objects, "
"`opaque`, `?T`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:104
msgid ""
"`[1, 2] == [1, 2]` is **false** — two cells. Identity is O(1) with no deep "
"walk; compare content where content is the contract (a loop, or a hash/eq "
"interface contract for keys). `?T == ?T` is slot identity: two `nil`s are "
"equal, a null and a box are not. See [Rc, dispose, and "
"identity](rc-dispose-identity.md) for the full law and the lint on "
"obviously-fresh composites."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:111
#: src/reference/typed-bytecode.md:121
msgid "`bytes.clone()` — the one copy escape hatch"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:113
msgid ""
"`b.clone() -> bytes` mints a fresh buffer with `b`'s octets — a one-shot "
"deep copy, the **only copy syntax in the language**. `bytes.from(a)` also "
"deep-copies. Every other type shares on binding, and a divergent value of "
"any other type is unreachable — build a new one instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:118
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let header = bytes.from([1, 2, 3]);\n"
"    let alias = header;              // shares: one buffer, two names\n"
"    let diverged = header.clone();   // a fresh buffer, same octets\n"
"    log.info(f\"{alias == header} {diverged == header}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:134
msgid "Containers under sharing"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:136
msgid ""
"`Vec<T>` backs on `buf: [?T]` — `[nil; cap]` is the generic zero (`nil` is "
"the slot's zero); `push` takes the `T → ?T`\\-boxed handle; loads yield the "
"`?T` (uses auto-deref). See [Builtin generic "
"types](builtin-generic-types.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:140
msgid ""
"Keyed collections: `get(k) -> ?V` — `nil` = absent, a hit answers the "
"**stored cell** (the aliasing law: two gets of one key name one cell until a "
"replace re-stores)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:143
msgid ""
"Array windows (`v.slice(a, b) -> ?Vec<T>`) are the shared cell: writes "
"through the window hit the parent (see [String slicing and "
"views](string-views.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:146
msgid ""
"Erasure rides the same laws: `opaque(v)` aliases the payload's cell, and "
"`opaque.downcast<T>(o)`'s match is the box's own inner cell (see [opaque — "
"erasure and downcast](opaque.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:150
msgid "The answer channel"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/by-reference-and-nullable.md:152
msgid ""
"**`(?T, err)` — concretely `(value, ok)` / `(T, str)` — is THE answer "
"channel.** The convention is law: empty err + a value = success; empty err + "
"`nil` = \"not found\"; non-empty err = \"failed\". The err channel "
"disambiguates a legitimately-absent value from a failure. The "
"exactly-one-non-nil invariant is the caller's convention — the engine types "
"the components and decodes them; it does not police the combination. A "
"return-position destructure (`let (v, ok) = f();`) is optimized so the pair "
"mints no cell where it is used as a pair."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:3
msgid ""
"rut's heap is **reference-counted**. When an object's strong count reaches "
"zero it is destroyed **immediately**, at a deterministic point in the "
"program. There is no collector: strong cycles leak by design — [weak "
"references](weak-refs.md) are the answer. Every VM owns one heap on one "
"thread and nothing is shared between isolates ([workers and "
"channels](workers-and-channels.md)), so the counters are plain cells with no "
"atomics and no locks."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:11
msgid "The cell model"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:13
msgid "Everything except primitives and `fn` values is a heap cell:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:15
msgid "Inline (moved by plain copy)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:15
msgid "Heap cells (refcounted handles)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:17
msgid "`u8..u64`, `i8..i64`, `u`/`isize`, `f32`, `f64`, `bool`, `nil`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:17
msgid ""
"`str`, `bytes`, `Vec<T>`, `[T]`, enums, structs, classes, interface objects, "
"`opaque` boxes, host boxes, `?T` boxes, coroutine frames"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:19
msgid ""
"Assignment, argument passing, and returning copy the **handle** (retain), "
"never the bytes. Mutation through one alias is visible through every alias — "
"reference semantics is the one default regime; there is no eager copy "
"anywhere. `bytes.clone()` is the **only** copy escape hatch."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:24
msgid ""
"There is no borrow syntax and no lifetimes: a handle simply keeps its "
"referent alive, so nothing dangles. Uniqueness matters only when "
"transferring buffers across isolates, where it is detected at runtime (`rc "
"== 1`) — never proven statically."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:29
msgid "Refcounting rules"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:31
msgid ""
"The compiler knows the static type of every register, so ref-aware ops are "
"emitted only where a reference can flow:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:34
msgid "Primitives and `fn` values move with a plain move — no counting."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:35
msgid ""
"References move with a ref move — retain the new, release the old; both "
"steps are exact."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:37
msgid ""
"Function boundaries pass references in registers; call/return sequences emit "
"the paired inc/dec. A loop over a `Vec<f64>` does no per-element counting."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:40
msgid ""
"**Overflow**: an increment past `u32::MAX` _immortalizes_ the object — the "
"count is pinned to the `0` sentinel (the same value interned literals use) — "
"a deliberate, logged leak instead of unsoundness."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:43
msgid ""
"Debug builds assert the full counter discipline: retain/release pairing, no "
"underflow, no double destruction."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:46
msgid "Destruction — the `[disposal]` marker"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:48
msgid ""
"A type opts into destructor code by marking an inherent member `[disposal]` "
"(see [Rc, dispose, and identity](rc-dispose-identity.md) for the marker's "
"surface — the name is free, the bracket designates):"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:52
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ DisposalContext };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Audit { log: Logger; n: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"impl Audit {\n"
"    [disposal] fn release(mut self, cx: DisposalContext) {\n"
"        self.log.info(f\"released {self.n} octets\");\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn work() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"work\");\n"
"    let buf = Audit { log: log, n: 4 };\n"
"    log.info(\"working — 4 octets in flight\");\n"
"}   // buf's count reaches zero here\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    work();   // the boundary drains: dispose runs after main's body\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:80 src/reference/launched-futures.md:28
#: src/reference/reflection.md:109
msgid "Laws:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:82
msgid ""
"The engine calls `dispose(mut self, cx: DisposalContext)` exactly once per "
"cell death — when the strong count reaches zero. Types without a "
"`[disposal]` member release straight to the field walk."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:85
msgid ""
"The call runs **at a call boundary**, not re-entrantly inside the release: "
"death pins the cell and queues it, and the interpreter drains the queue at "
"its call boundaries — the root call's end for sync code, each drive for the "
"async weave — passing the pinned cell as `self` with a freshly minted `cx`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:90
msgid ""
"A dispose body may itself release references (a field walk that kills a "
"child whose own `dispose` is next): the drain loops until the queue stays "
"empty."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:93
msgid ""
"The context `cx` is engine-minted and empty today; it grows additively, "
"never by touching the marker's signature."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:95
msgid ""
"Cancellation drops locals at the suspension point through the same machinery "
"([launched futures](launched-futures.md)) — no special case."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:98
msgid ""
"Note the difference from finalizer-based runtimes: `dispose` always runs, "
"exactly once, at a knowable point. There is no \"later or never\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:101
msgid "Destruction order"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:103
msgid "When a cell's strong count reaches zero:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:105
msgid ""
"Every `Weak` box watching the cell is nulled **before any user code runs** — "
"a dispose body that calls `upgrade()` sees `nil`, deterministically ([weak "
"references](weak-refs.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:108
msgid ""
"The type's queued `dispose` body runs for cells whose type carries a "
"`[disposal]` member (pinned cell, then released)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:110
msgid ""
"Ref-typed children are released **recursively**: record fields in "
"declaration order, sum payloads, array elements, `opaque` box inners, "
"closure captures. The walk is driven by a per-type release plan built once "
"from the type table."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:114
msgid ""
"Host box payloads run their Rust `Drop` at the same point — sockets, files, "
"and textures die with the last handle, not \"sometime later\". An opt-in "
"`finalize` hook (no-op default) runs _before_ the payload's `Drop`; a rut "
"value the payload held releases through the same walk."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:118
msgid ""
"The block store frees the cell's variable-size payload; the slot returns to "
"the arena free list and is reused by the next mint ([the VM "
"heap](vm-heap.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:122
msgid "Buffers, strings, and views"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:124
msgid ""
"**Primitive-element `[T]` buffers stay flat**: a `[T]` over numeric or "
"`bool` element types stores raw values inline behind the header. Element "
"copies in and out are plain moves — no counting. This is the one place the "
"everything-is-a-cell law does not reach."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:128
msgid ""
"**`Vec<T>` is not flat**: its backing is `buf: [?T]`, so element traffic "
"crosses nullable handles. `push`/`pop`/`set` emit the paired retain/release."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:131
msgid ""
"**Composite-element buffers store handles**: `[Point]` and `Vec<Point>` hold "
"one cell pointer per element; the buffer itself is the counted unit."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:134
msgid ""
"`[T]` is a fixed-length cell; `N` is a compile-time constant and part of the "
"type's identity. Fixed-array literals that fold at compile time become "
"immortal constant-pool cells."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:137
msgid ""
"**`Slice<T>` view cells** hold a handle to their owner plus `off`/`len` — "
"never a pointer into the data block. Views dispatch through the owner, so "
"growth keeps existing views valid; indexing bounds-checks against `len ∩ "
"owner len` and **traps** out of range instead of reading garbage. Slices are "
"born only from implicit widening at an argument site; they are not nameable "
"or constructible in script."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:143
msgid ""
"**`str` is immutable**: interned literals live in the module's constant pool "
"(immortal); runtime-built strings are ordinary cells whose buffers are "
"internally copy-on-write — an implementation detail no program can observe, "
"because `str` has no mutation API."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:147
msgid ""
"**No interior pointers exist anywhere**, which is what keeps every heap walk "
"a simple typed walk."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:150
msgid "Internals"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:152
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"Header:  rc: u32        // strong count; u32::MAX = immortal sentinel\n"
"         ty: u32        // index into the type table\n"
"         bytes: u32     // accounted bytes, refunded on release\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:158
msgid ""
"The weak lists (referent slot word → its `WeakBox` cells) and the "
"disposal-armed markers (cells whose `dispose` is queued or has run) are "
"arena side tables, not header words."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:162
msgid ""
"Cell kinds: string, vec (growable), array (fixed), slice view, record (every "
"user struct/class — vtable + payload slots), enum (tag + payload slots; "
"dataless variants are immortal singletons), opaque box, weak box, and the "
"coroutine frames the async weave mints. Reified layouts are covered in "
"[reified types and layout](reified-types.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:168
msgid "The engine's two counting ops are exact and total:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:171
msgid "/* rc += 1 */"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:174
msgid "// underflow is a bug: assert\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:176
msgid "// order above; no suspect list\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/rc-heap.md:180
msgid ""
"Destruction is fully deterministic under the virtual clock, so a drop order "
"reproduces exactly in tests. Identity and equality semantics for cell values "
"are covered in [Rc, dispose, and identity](rc-dispose-identity.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:3
msgid ""
"Reference counting frees an object when its strong count reaches zero ([the "
"Rc heap](rc-heap.md)). A strong cycle never reaches zero — so **strong "
"cycles leak until the `Vm` is dropped**. rut states this as a rule instead "
"of hiding it behind a collector: the heap stays trivially simple, "
"destructors stay exactly deterministic, and `Weak<T>` gives the two shapes "
"that cause accidental cycles — back-pointers and caches — a first-class "
"answer."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:11
msgid "Cycle policy"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:13
msgid "Rule"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:13
msgid "Content"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:15
msgid "No collector"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:15
msgid ""
"No mark bits, no colors, no pauses beyond destructor chains. What leaks "
"leaks wholly and predictably."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:16
msgid "Resource holders"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:16
msgid ""
"Classes holding resources (`[disposal]` members, host boxes) must **not** "
"participate in strong cycles."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:17
msgid "Back-pointers"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:17
msgid "Parent/child and observer shapes take a `Weak` back-pointer."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:18
msgid "Pure-data cycles"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:18
msgid "Harmless — memory only, freed wholesale at teardown."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:19
msgid "Lints"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:19
msgid ""
"The compiler may warn on obvious self-reference (a value stored into its own "
"field through a handle path); general cycle detection stays out of scope."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:21
msgid ""
"Cycles are possible through ordinary struct fields (`next: ?Node` "
"back-pointers) as well as through collections."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:24 src/reference/stdlib.md:77
msgid "`Weak<T>`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:26
msgid ""
"`Weak` is a builtin generic class with one member. A weak box is a side cell "
"holding the referent's slot word, **unretained** — a weak never keeps "
"anything alive, and a path through a `Weak` does not close a strong cycle."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:31
msgid ""
"`Weak` is one of core's **import-gated** builtin names: spell `use core::{ "
"Weak };` to bring the class in ([core and the swappable "
"packages](stdlib.md)). A bare `Weak` does not compile — the diagnostic is `` "
"`Weak` is not in scope — `use core::{ Weak }` ``."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:37
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ Weak };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"class Node {\n"
"    value: u32;\n"
"    next: ?Node;          // strong — keeps the tail alive\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Node {\n"
"    pub fn new(value: u32) -> Self { return Self { value: value, next: nil "
"}; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"fn use_node(n: Node) {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    log.info(f\"node {n.value}\");\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let n = Node.new(1);\n"
"    let w = Weak.new(n);        // Weak<Node>; T infers from n\n"
"    let b = w.upgrade();        // ?Node — a live handle\n"
"    if (b != nil) {\n"
"        use_node(b);\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:69
msgid "Construction — `Weak.new(v)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:71
msgid ""
"Construction is a **class method** with admission at the instantiation: `T` "
"must be a **reference type**. `Weak<i32>` and `Weak.new(some_fn)` diagnose; "
"primitives and `fn` values refuse."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:74
msgid ""
"Works over **any cell**: a class, struct, `Vec`, `[T]`, enum, `str`, "
"`bytes`, a user `opaque` box, or a host box."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:76
msgid "`Weak.new(nil)` traps (\"weak on nil\")."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:77
msgid ""
"**`Weak.new(v)` consumes the argument's temporary** and nulls its register "
"(the engine's one consuming op): the weak observes the _binding's_ lifetime, "
"never a temporary's. `Weak.new(make())` watches a referent that dies as soon "
"as the temporary is released — `upgrade()` answers `nil`. Bind the value "
"first:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:83
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ Weak };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Payload { n: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"fn make() -> Payload { return Payload { n: 1 }; }\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let v = make();\n"
"    let w = Weak.new(v);  // watches the binding v — lives as long as v "
"does\n"
"    log.info(f\"{w.upgrade() != nil}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:103
msgid ""
"Two `Weak.new(v)` of one `v` are distinct boxes; `==` on weak boxes is "
"identity, and two weaks of the same referent are not equal to each other."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:107
msgid "Upgrade — `w.upgrade() -> ?T`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:109
msgid ""
"A dead-check plus retain: a live referent is retained into a fresh `?T` "
"handle; a dead one answers the **null slot** — true `nil`, never a box "
"containing nil."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:112
msgid ""
"On `Weak<?U>` (legal) the answer is `??U` — the sticky-`?` law "
"([by-reference and nullable](by-reference-and-nullable.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:114
msgid ""
"`upgrade()` on nil traps (\"upgrade on nil\"); on a non-weak value it traps "
"(\"upgrade on non-weak\")."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:117
msgid "Deterministic ordering"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:119
msgid ""
"The referent's death **nulls every weak box before anything that runs user "
"code** — before the dispose pin, before payload teardown. A dispose body "
"that calls `upgrade()` sees `nil`, with no window:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/weak-refs.md:123
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ DisposalContext, Weak };\n"
"\n"
"class Node  { child: ?Node; }\n"
"class Child { back: ?Weak<Node>; }   // the observer's weak back-pointer\n"
"\n"
"impl Child {\n"
"    [disposal] fn release(mut self, cx: DisposalContext) {\n"
"        // the parent's death released self through the field walk,\n"
"        // and the parent's weak list was nulled before any of that\n"
"        // user code ran — the back-pointer reads nil from in here:\n"
"        if (self.back.upgrade() == nil) { /* always taken here */ }\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
