#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:20
msgid "The receipt — `LaunchedFutureHandle<T>`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:30
msgid ""
"The receipt is **not a `Future`**: `await h` diagnoses — the type system "
"rejects it, never a runtime check."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:32
msgid ""
"It is **not re-launchable**: `launch_future(h)` is a type error for the same "
"reason. A future is consumed exactly once — by `await` or by "
"`launch_future`, never both, never twice."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:35
msgid ""
"One member: `abort()` — `true` if the frame was flagged and re-enqueued, "
"`false` when it had already finished (a repeat abort is stable and harmless)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:39
msgid "Cancellation"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:41
msgid ""
"`abort()` flags the frame's cancellation and re-enqueues it. **The probe at "
"its next checkpoint is the only place cancellation becomes observable** — it "
"never interrupts mid-expression."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:45
msgid "At the probe, the frame runs its **drop path**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:47
msgid "the pending edge is cleared — a pending `sleep` dies with the frame;"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:48
msgid ""
"every local releases **deterministically, in reverse declaration order** "
"(`[disposal]` members run, [the Rc heap](rc-heap.md));"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:50
msgid "the state retires (null) — later `abort()` calls answer `false`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:52
msgid ""
"Inside an async body, the injected `cx.cancelled()` reads the same flag as "
"data, so a frame can honor cancellation at its own pace. Cancellation is "
"synchronous at the engine level (flag + re-enqueue); a frame parked on a "
"host future only observes the flag when the loop drives it again."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:58
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ DisposalContext };\n"
"\n"
"class Drops {\n"
"    n: u32;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"impl Drops {\n"
"    [disposal] fn release(mut self, cx: DisposalContext) { self.n += 1; }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"async fn job() -> nil {\n"
"    let buf = Drops { n: 0 };\n"
"    await sleep(5000);\n"
"    // if `job` is aborted while parked here, the probe at the sleep\n"
"    // checkpoint runs the drop path: buf's `[disposal]` member fires,\n"
"    // then the locals release in reverse declaration order\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:78
msgid "Join (specified)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:80
msgid ""
"The receipt is the join surface: `await h` joins the launched future and "
"produces its completion value. Until the join tier lands, `await h` "
"diagnoses with the join law and the receipt stays non-awaitable."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:85
msgid ""
"Planned laws: joining an already-cancelled receipt yields the cancelled "
"case, never a trap; join re-enqueues the awaiter the same way an in-body "
"`await` parks, so the value surfaces on the driving loop, not through a "
"callback."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:90
msgid "Racing — `select2` / `select_all` (stdlib)"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:92
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"use futures::{ launch_future, sleep, select2, Either2 };\n"
"\n"
"async fn slow_fetch() -> str {            // the stand-in for any slow "
"producer\n"
"    await sleep(200);\n"
"    return \"data\";\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"async fn fetch_or_timeout() -> str {\n"
"    let winner = await select2(slow_fetch(), sleep(50));\n"
"    if (winner.is_a()) {\n"
"        return winner.a_value();\n"
"    }\n"
"    return \"timeout\";\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"async fn run_race(log: Logger) -> nil {\n"
"    log.info(await fetch_or_timeout());   // \"timeout\" — sleep(50) wins "
"the race\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"race\");\n"
"    launch_future(run_race(log));\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:119
msgid ""
"`select2(a, b)` races two futures; the call **mints the race** — nothing "
"runs until `await` (or `launch_future`) drives it. The answer is `Either2<T, "
"U>`: `is_a()`/`a_value()` read the first future's side, `is_b()`/`b_value()` "
"the second's. The winner's type is the fn signature's `Either2<T, U>` — "
"computed at the call site, never a cast."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:125
msgid ""
"The **losers are cancelled** the moment a winner is settled: their drop "
"paths run (pending sleeps die with them, `[disposal]` members fire). A "
"cancelled race (`h.abort()` on the race future) cascades through its "
"children."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:129
msgid ""
"`select_all<T>(futs)` races a whole `[Future<T>]` cohort and resolves with "
"`(i, v)` — the winner's index and value — cancelling every other still-live "
"member. Ties (two members already retired) break by index order."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:134
msgid ""
"`Either2` is an ordinary generic class (`left: ?A; right: ?B`) — rut's enums "
"are the C-style member set, so the two-sided answer is private fields plus "
"accessors, not pattern arms."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:138
msgid "Manual futures — `completer<T>()`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:140
msgid ""
"`completer<T>()` mints a cold `Future<T>` **and** the resolution right "
"beside it — the primitive that turns a plain callback into a future the "
"whole `await`/`select2` vocabulary drives:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:144
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use core::{ Future };\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"use futures::{ completer, Completer, launch_future, sleep };\n"
"\n"
"// a PLAIN fn returning a Future; no async block needed:\n"
"fn fetch_like(tag: str) -> Future<str> {\n"
"    let (f, done) = completer<str>();\n"
"    launch_future(settle_later(done, tag));   // the \"callback\" side\n"
"    return f;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"async fn settle_later(done: Completer<str>, tag: str) -> nil {\n"
"    await sleep(10);\n"
"    done.resolve(tag);\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"async fn consume(log: Logger) -> nil {\n"
"    let v = await fetch_like(\"tag!\");         // parks until the resolve\n"
"    log.info(v);\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"entry fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"completer\");\n"
"    launch_future(consume(log));\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:172
msgid ""
"`done.resolve(v)` settles the future and wakes its awaiter; it answers "
"`false` when the future already retired — resolved, cancelled, or abandoned "
"(the same stability `abort()` has). The right consumes itself: one resolve."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:176
msgid ""
"`Completer<T>` is only the **right**, not the future — a "
"`LaunchedFutureHandle` sibling. `await` diagnoses on it; the engine minted "
"future it settles is the awaitable."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:180
msgid "Structure and fairness"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:182
msgid ""
"v1 launched futures are **unstructured**: aborting a frame does not abort "
"frames it awaits. Structured scopes — `scope { .. }` cancelling children on "
"exit — are the specified remedy."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:185
msgid ""
"The ready ring is **round-robin**: each drive runs a frame to its next park "
"or completion, so one greedy future cannot starve the queue. Priorities are "
"not in the model."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:188
msgid ""
"`vm.pending_tasks()` counts ready frames, armed timers, and host futures "
"parked on Completers — the embedder's idle test ([async and "
"await](async.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/launched-futures.md:191
msgid ""
"A trap inside a launched future propagates out of `run_ready()` / `drive()`; "
"the frame is retired either way, and its locals ran their drop path."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/host-futures.md:3
msgid ""
"Native Rust code can back a rut async fn. The contract is one closure per "
"operation: spawn the work, hand out a **`Completer<T>`**, and settle it from "
"anywhere. The engine weaves the future; the embedder never touches a frame."
msgstr ""

